WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.760
Tim Ferriss: Apollo.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.227
Apollo Robbins: Yes, sir.

00:00:17.227 --> 00:00:18.384
Tim Ferriss: So nice to have you here.

00:00:18.384 --> 00:00:19.627
Apollo Robbins: It is a pleasure.

00:00:19.627 --> 00:00:22.960
Tim Ferriss: And this has been&nbsp;
in the works for me, at least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:26.904
in my own head since I want to say 2018, 2019.

00:00:26.904 --> 00:00:27.187
Apollo Robbins: Wow.

00:00:27.187 --> 00:00:32.440
Tim Ferriss: And then this pesky thing that we now&nbsp;
call COVID came along and disrupted all sorts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:37.480
plans, but finally, here we are. So thank you for&nbsp;
making the trip, and I'm glad that you are here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:41.840
in person, so we may get a chance to stand up.
Apollo Robbins: It's nice that it's in person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:43.680
versus virtual. There's so&nbsp;
many more possibilities.&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:50.960
Tim Ferriss: So many more possibilities. Well,&nbsp;
let's start. So people might assume that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:56.440
right out of the gate, maybe as a fetus,&nbsp;
but certainly, once you entered the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:07.640
you had the dexterity of a demigod and were&nbsp;
basically fated to become someone famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:14.560
for how they use their hands. Is that the case?
Apollo Robbins: No. Quite the opposite. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:20.560
almost as opposite as it could be on that. This&nbsp;
is a story I've been told, that once upon a time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:26.240
my father, who was legally blind, had decided&nbsp;
to become a minister, went to seminary in Enid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:32.040
Oklahoma, and was walking down the streets from&nbsp;
seminary, but he had moved into this kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:35.920
rough neighborhood, was trying to witness,&nbsp;
and these two teenagers decided to try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.920
steal from him. He didn't know what they were up&nbsp;
to, but then, when they realized he was blind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:44.800
they decided not to hustle him.
But instead, they tried to guide him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:50.680
to where the bus was and encouraged him to go to&nbsp;
another area. And he invited them to his church,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:55.880
invited them to have food on a Wednesday night,&nbsp;
and they came and they did. They found food and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:00.160
they brought their mom one time. And then,&nbsp;
their mom met him and they got married,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:06.400
and that's supposedly how my parents met.
Tim Ferriss: Wow. Okay. So, your parents meet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.827
one thing leads to another.
Apollo Robbins: Yes. It's —&nbsp;

00:02:08.827 --> 00:02:11.664
Tim Ferriss: I'm assuming&nbsp;
no immaculate conception.&nbsp;

00:02:11.664 --> 00:02:15.280
Apollo Robbins: Perhaps.
Tim Ferriss: And then, you enter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:20.520
the world, what were doctors expectations of you?
Apollo Robbins: They were upside down. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:25.440
my mother had had previous children,&nbsp;
she had three and she was a widow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:31.080
they were also in their teens, they assumed that&nbsp;
I had the same father. So they didn't test their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:36.160
blood. So there was a factor in the blood, I&nbsp;
think it was an RH factor, but it created what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:41.920
they call ABO blood disease. So it required,&nbsp;
when I was born, that I had a transfusion.&nbsp;

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:45.960
There were a few other things. My mom had lupus,&nbsp;
connective tissue disease, which created tumors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:52.400
in her uterus, and so my legs developed around&nbsp;
those, so they were twisted up. And I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:57.680
out of that — I mean, it's an interesting —&nbsp;
we can go in this further, but out of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:02.120
when I was born, there was a product of a little,&nbsp;
the Forrest Gump thing, that I had to wear braces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:05.880
on my legs like Forrest Gump to get around.
But also, I had fine and gross motor problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:10.160
I couldn't control my hands. It was kind of the&nbsp;
joke of trying to clap the hands three times at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:13.600
the therapist. I was a little bit better than&nbsp;
that. I can clap my hands, but I couldn't do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:19.480
a lot more. I had to, when I learned to write,&nbsp;
have a large diameter pencil that was through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:23.280
a rubber ball, and I had to learn to write that&nbsp;
with both hands up until about the second grade.&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:29.160
So I had a lot of therapy, which I think that&nbsp;
idea of persistent learning through overcoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:35.707
an obstacle like that has helped me be resilient&nbsp;
with acquiring a lot of skills over my lifetime.&nbsp;

00:03:35.707 --> 00:03:44.600
Tim Ferriss: Could you say more about your father?&nbsp;
So, was he blind for your entire life? Did that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:52.160
have some onset? And how did that shape who he&nbsp;
was? And of course, the implicit question is how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:54.859
did that affect you, but let's start with —
Apollo Robbins: Yeah.&nbsp;

00:03:54.859 --> 00:03:56.880
Tim Ferriss: — the simpler version&nbsp;
of the question. Could you just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:03.880
tell us more about your father?
Apollo Robbins: He was a short man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:09.160
and I think that that factors in, because he was&nbsp;
about five foot four. So, it's pretty — quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:18.120
short. He was very physically fit. He climbed&nbsp;
rope without using his legs. He was very quick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:22.600
to go to a fight when he was younger.
And then, one time, when he was going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:30.080
into college, he was studying to be a CPA,&nbsp;
and he had some kind of bout that — he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:34.680
tuberculous meningitis that affected him for&nbsp;
about a year, year and a half. It was a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:38.880
serious disease at the time. I mean, still&nbsp;
is, but at the time, it was more pervasive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:47.040
and it made him blind during his productive year,&nbsp;
and he became kind of born again. Somebody had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.960
come to visit him and witnessed to him during&nbsp;
that time, and he decided he was going to change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:57.080
the course of his life, become a minister.
And that, I think, made him a very dogmatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.080 --> 00:05:01.960
minister, too. He really embodied himself with&nbsp;
thinking about the difference of Paul versus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:09.760
Saul in the Bible. And when he came out — he was&nbsp;
a traveling minister for most of my early life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:14.640
He would travel, visit churches. And some of the&nbsp;
things — I think one of the creative parts that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:20.720
really took from him was that he would travel to a&nbsp;
church not knowing what his presentation was going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:25.400
to be, but he would find an animal along the way,&nbsp;
a turtle crossing the road, and that became his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.600
presentation. He would sit it in front of the&nbsp;
pulpit and everybody would circle around him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:33.360
and would talk about the turtle as a analogy or a&nbsp;
metaphor. And he was able to connect these ideas.&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:38.800
And meanwhile, I would sit in the back with my&nbsp;
little braces, and usually, a pen and paper and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:44.360
try to draw everything that was going on. Art was&nbsp;
a big thing for me, and it had been for him before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:49.880
he had lost his sight. So I think those influences&nbsp;
factor in later to some of the other things. But I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:54.680
think one of the bigger takeaways wasn't the blind&nbsp;
spots he had through vision, although it did help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:59.120
that when we would walk around, I would try to be&nbsp;
his eyes for him, there was this tug of war that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:03.920
he didn't want anybody to know that he was really&nbsp;
blind. He didn't often emphasize that. He would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:11.292
often walk with a confidence level that was —
Tim Ferriss: Ill-suited to his eyesight.&nbsp;

00:06:11.292 --> 00:06:15.280
Apollo Robbins: Yeah. Yeah. Yeah. And to his&nbsp;
chagrin, because he would run full force into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:22.280
a park bench and come tumbling over it. But&nbsp;
he created this ministry that was inside of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:26.760
government subsidized housing and what people&nbsp;
think as the hood. And he would get beat up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:31.800
in front of me, by gangs, sometimes, or not&nbsp;
necessarily gangs, but just small groups. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:33.800
guess you'd call gangs. And it was —
Tim Ferriss: Where was this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:35.280
again? Can you remind me?
Apollo Robbins: It's in the — well, now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:38.344
it's branded as a TV show, the Ozarks.
Tim Ferriss: Ahh. Okay.&nbsp;

00:06:38.344 --> 00:06:41.840
Apollo Robbins: But the city was Springfield,&nbsp;
Missouri. And he would go into those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:45.880
neighborhoods. He would go, he would set&nbsp;
up his ministry inside of a laundromat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:50.520
and he would draw the kids in with a story. He'd&nbsp;
bring Kool-Aid and cookies every Sunday. He did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:54.000
that for 20 years. And they would come in and he&nbsp;
would tell them these stories. It meant a lot to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:58.640
him. He was non-denominational, but he was very&nbsp;
dogmatic about his specific interpretation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:03.760
scripture, which, to me, myself, jumping&nbsp;
ahead, I'm agnostic. I am not an atheist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:09.360
but I'm not subscribed to any religion.
But it was such an interesting learning lesson. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:13.680
think about, the difference between — most people&nbsp;
would judge somebody by their perceptual blind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:18.080
spots of not being able to see, but sometimes,&nbsp;
it's worse, the stories that we tell ourselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:20.720
and how that limits our perception.
Tim Ferriss: We'll definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:22.120
come back to this.
Apollo Robbins: Okay.&nbsp;

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:25.960
Tim Ferriss: Like perception,&nbsp;
story, perception-shaping story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:32.360
story-shaping perception, I want to talk a lot&nbsp;
more about this. But since it's something you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:41.240
just mentioned, the agnosticism, did you start off&nbsp;
religious, and then, at some point along the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:47.800
become agnostic? Was it a response to,&nbsp;
perhaps, your father's dogmatism? How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:53.040
did you get to self-identifying in that way?
Apollo Robbins: I started off as religious, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:57.280
I think is the same thing, people start off as&nbsp;
extremely patriotic to their certain country where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:02.520
they're born or where you go to form a family,&nbsp;
where they're very religious, it'll be under that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:09.760
flag as well. And my father had me study a lot&nbsp;
of different types of scriptures, from Jehovah's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:13.360
Witness to the Mormons, because he wanted to&nbsp;
be able to debate those for a lot of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:20.040
topics. He had the book of Hebrew, he wanted me to&nbsp;
study King James for its specific interpretation.&nbsp;

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:26.960
I had a debate in debate groups for evolution&nbsp;
and creationism, so I was on teams that did that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:34.920
I was in a bible quiz team, where I would buzz&nbsp;
these buzzers and quote scriptures, and I went to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:38.160
the world championships in that and placed in the&nbsp;
world championships. So it's all these things as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:44.280
a young kid that I was deeply entrenched in that&nbsp;
world, and there was just poking at this thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:51.160
that this certainty is what flagged me,&nbsp;
just the absoluteness of my father's answer.&nbsp;

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:55.880
And it was in juxtaposition of what was happening&nbsp;
on the other side of my family. So my mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.880 --> 00:09:00.720
and this shifts to the other side. It was a&nbsp;
dichotomy between the two, because she had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:10.160
a widow and she had raised these three kids that&nbsp;
— her husband had died very early, and two of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:16.800
had picked up this beyond a hobby of how they were&nbsp;
making money on the other side of the law, from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:21.080
stealing things, running small hustles. They never&nbsp;
called it pickpocketing, but they would steal from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:26.120
people at the zoo when they were leaning over to&nbsp;
watch things, animals being fed, they would cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:32.120
their pockets or steal their bags, at sporting&nbsp;
events through bleachers, mostly, opportunistic.&nbsp;

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:38.080
But later, it evolved. In the military, when&nbsp;
he came out, he started working, again like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:42.680
series, Ozark, I feel like that route that was in&nbsp;
Ozark is probably what my brothers are associated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:46.858
with, with some type of trafficking group `—
Tim Ferriss: Drug trafficking.&nbsp;

00:09:46.858 --> 00:09:51.200
Apollo Robbins: Yeah, drug trafficking up&nbsp;
through that pipeline. One of my brothers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:57.720
also did smuggling for firearms, so moving&nbsp;
those in and out. So it became very intense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.720 --> 00:10:01.360
and I was exposed to some of that at the same&nbsp;
time that I was growing up with my father as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:05.280
devout minister. So it was really, I think a&nbsp;
fascinating juxtaposition between those two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:12.360
worlds, that seeing deception in that true sense&nbsp;
of the word of what we often think it to be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:17.320
but yet, also, he was okay with my brothers&nbsp;
as people. He didn't know that that's what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:21.800
they did. But I got to see both sides of that.
And they did other things publicly. They were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:27.200
truck drivers or other things, and exceptional&nbsp;
people in many ways, but it taught me to see the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:32.240
gray. I think back to your other question now,&nbsp;
became — when I started to go to the church,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:36.200
I'd sneak out of the back of the church when&nbsp;
the services transition, and I'd sneak off to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:40.320
another church and say, well, if the Assemblies&nbsp;
of God believe that tongues is the first physical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:44.560
initial sign of baptism in the Holy Spirit,&nbsp;
then what do the Baptists believe? And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:47.520
then I'd go see their tenets.
And then, as I bounced around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:52.320
I noticed that there were just lots of little&nbsp;
slices of different views or perspectives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:57.480
which, to me, has continued to follow me through&nbsp;
my life of anytime somebody claims a truth is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.480 --> 00:10:59.400
maybe we need more perspectives on this.
Tim Ferriss:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:05.547
When does Ben Stone enter the picture?
Apollo Robbins: Wow, that's a great name.&nbsp;

00:11:05.547 --> 00:11:08.000
Tim Ferriss: And how old are you?
Apollo Robbins: How dark do you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:12.504
to get? I can take it light or dark or gray.
Tim Ferriss: Maybe you pick, dealer's choice.&nbsp;

00:11:12.504 --> 00:11:14.920
Apollo Robbins: Okay.
Tim Ferriss: I mean, we've gone dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:18.640
on this podcast before, so we can go there, but —
Apollo Robbins: I'll pop in and out. So, just as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:24.720
transition, once upon a time, I had had a pursuit&nbsp;
to become a syndicated cartoon artist. And I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:30.720
about 12, 13, 14, going through that. And I was&nbsp;
pretty confident that I could pull it off. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:36.880
wanted to be the youngest one. There was a guy who&nbsp;
was imitating Gary Larson's single-panel cartoons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:41.600
and I had six months of character sheets. I&nbsp;
was going to send them out to get syndicated.&nbsp;

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:45.240
And my parents didn't understand what this was,&nbsp;
or what this weird thing I was doing. They just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:49.720
knew I liked to draw and do cartoons. But&nbsp;
I think it's important to understand the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:54.480
energy of what that was. And then, there was a&nbsp;
fight, and parents have fights with their kids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.480 --> 00:12:00.040
with their teens. It's just, mine was a little bit&nbsp;
more intense. My father thought I was possessed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.040 --> 00:12:03.664
So they tried to evoke a demon out of me.
Tim Ferriss: Because of the cartooning?&nbsp;

00:12:03.664 --> 00:12:06.368
Apollo Robbins: Not because of the cartooning,&nbsp;
but because of some argument that we had.&nbsp;

00:12:06.368 --> 00:12:07.120
Tim Ferriss: Ah.
Apollo Robbins: But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:13.680
the argument — I say this as proximity to&nbsp;
my large case of six months of cartoons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:19.720
my parents — some variation of a water boarding&nbsp;
process occurred and the water poured over on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.720 --> 00:12:26.400
all my art. So that's the dark side, that&nbsp;
it destroyed all the art. And when you had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:30.760
that much work invested, it's a reset. How do you&nbsp;
start over? What do you do? And for me as a kid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.760 --> 00:12:33.800
that was what I pictured as my escape.
Tim Ferriss: The cartooning was going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:35.560
to be your escape plan?
Apollo Robbins: Yes. Yeah.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.560 --> 00:12:39.200
Mm-hmm. From that world. And then, I think, I&nbsp;
was down in our basement, I was going through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:45.080
some boxes and I found this little magic gadget.&nbsp;
It was a piece of plastic, looked like a thumb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.080 --> 00:12:48.960
and I was very curious about what that was. And&nbsp;
somebody said, "It's a magic trick." And I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:53.000
"Well, how did that end up in our stuff?"
So I call a magic shop that was downtown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:55.960
and there's this guy picked up the phone&nbsp;
and he was probably 70 something at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.960 --> 00:12:59.680
time. His name was Ben Stone. So, long&nbsp;
way around to answer your question.&nbsp;

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:05.080
And Ben Stone, I said, so I've got this&nbsp;
plastic thumb, I don't know what it is. I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.080 --> 00:13:09.680
"Do I act like my finger's cut off? What do&nbsp;
I do?" I'm like 14. I'm very precocious. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.680 --> 00:13:14.360
borderline at this choice in my life, like I'm&nbsp;
shoplifting. I'm running away from home for two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:18.440
weeks at a time living out in the woods,&nbsp;
sometimes. So I'm kind of at a transition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.440 --> 00:13:22.920
place of which way I choose as my paths in life.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, that's a vulnerable place where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:25.307
you could have inflection points.
Apollo Robbins: Absolutely.&nbsp;

00:13:25.307 --> 00:13:27.144
Tim Ferriss: Or vectors in a&nbsp;
lot of different directions.&nbsp;

00:13:27.144 --> 00:13:30.800
Apollo Robbins: And I had lots of ingredients to&nbsp;
go a certain way. Later, as I've met people in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:36.800
my life, I resonated with them because of that,&nbsp;
because I had that fork to make a decision. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:40.067
Ben had a big impact at that decision, and that's&nbsp;
why I wanted to give you the context for it.&nbsp;

00:13:40.067 --> 00:13:40.783
Tim Ferriss: For sure.
Apollo Robbins: Yeah.&nbsp;

00:13:40.783 --> 00:13:43.240
Tim Ferriss: And I appreciate&nbsp;
that. And so what happens then?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:46.480
You're like, "I have this fake thumb."
Apollo Robbins: "What do I do?" Ben says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:51.120
"Come to the magic shop, downtown Springfield."&nbsp;
It was about 12 miles from wherever my house was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:55.960
So I rode a bike, I went with a friend,&nbsp;
and went down there. And he didn't show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.960 --> 00:14:01.947
me anything about the fake thumb, but he used&nbsp;
it as a trap. He made this coin move around —&nbsp;

00:14:01.947 --> 00:14:04.800
Tim Ferriss: Your kind of guy.
Apollo Robbins: Yeah, which is great. It's exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:09.280
what should have happened to me. And he moved this&nbsp;
sleight of hand with the coin, he made it jump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:15.280
around, disappeared, jumped through his clothing,&nbsp;
jumped through his body. I was fascinated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.280 --> 00:14:19.680
because I was thinking, if this old guy can do&nbsp;
this, I could probably pull this off myself.&nbsp;

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:24.240
And then, he pulled out a deck of cards called&nbsp;
the Svengali deck, and he did a bunch of tricks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:29.520
with that. He says, "So here's a choice." Kind of&nbsp;
like a red pill, blue pill. "This deck of cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:34.880
you can walk out of here, and for $5, you'll be&nbsp;
able to do 50 tricks without even practicing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:39.600
or here's this big book and it's going to take&nbsp;
a lot of study. And maybe in four or five years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:44.080
you'll be able to do sleight of hand with&nbsp;
almost anything, but it's your choice."&nbsp;

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:49.240
And it was his way of vetting at that point, I&nbsp;
think. And I didn't have money either way. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:55.120
wasn't the plan. So I went back, rode my bike back&nbsp;
to my house, and I sold some stuff at a pawn shop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.120 --> 00:14:59.760
and came back the next day and bought that&nbsp;
book. And for the first year, I just spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:04.080
time by myself studying the coin magic by myself.
Tim Ferriss: So I want to pause because we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:09.664
going to pick up on this particular thread. But&nbsp;
I want to rewind, I think, just a few years.&nbsp;

00:15:09.664 --> 00:15:10.960
Apollo Robbins: Sure.
Tim Ferriss: You're around 14,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.960 --> 00:15:13.427
15 at this point?
Apollo Robbins: Yeah.&nbsp;

00:15:13.427 --> 00:15:17.800
Tim Ferriss: So, I think this takes place&nbsp;
a bit earlier. You mentioned your father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:22.264
and the animals that, then, become&nbsp;
the parable or part of the sermon.&nbsp;

00:15:22.264 --> 00:15:24.880
Apollo Robbins: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: You can't believe everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:30.184
you read on the internet, but is it true that&nbsp;
you also used to take in orphaned animals?&nbsp;

00:15:30.184 --> 00:15:32.680
Apollo Robbins: I did.
Tim Ferriss: Okay. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.680 --> 00:15:34.400
can you tell me more about this?
Apollo Robbins: Wow. You have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.400 --> 00:15:38.406
research department. That's great. Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Got teams everywhere.&nbsp;

00:15:38.406 --> 00:15:39.944
Apollo Robbins: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Eyes and ears on the ground.&nbsp;

00:15:39.944 --> 00:15:41.840
Apollo Robbins: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: And so I'm curious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:49.680
how that came to be, and I don't want to make&nbsp;
it too leading of a question, but how that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:54.080
informed, or how it reflects who you are?
Apollo Robbins: Well, it's very salient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.080 --> 00:16:01.800
I think. When I was eight or so, I guess,&nbsp;
earlier, when I would take walks with my father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:08.400
he couldn't see things, but he would stop me when&nbsp;
we'd go for a hike through the woods or something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:14.120
and he'd say, "If you look around you right now,&nbsp;
within five foot, there's a world. There's lots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.120 --> 00:16:17.840
of little worlds. There's little communities of&nbsp;
things that are happening. You just have to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:24.480
them. So find them and tell me about them."
And that was a big thing. I saw a lizard on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:30.000
tree, and he said, "Walk straight to it and it's&nbsp;
going to run around the tree. When it does, reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:34.600
straight around behind it and cup your hand."&nbsp;
And he taught me that I could catch a lizard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:38.320
by walking around the tree because it'd go to the&nbsp;
same position. There's kind of a pattern to that.&nbsp;

00:16:38.320 --> 00:16:43.320
And he taught me this perspective taking for&nbsp;
animals I think helped a lot. It's a variation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.320 --> 00:16:51.840
of empathy that I think transferred later on for&nbsp;
me. But I had, at that time, squirrels, raccoons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:55.800
I babysit a bear for a weekend, a baby bear.
Tim Ferriss: Hold on. Let's take the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.800 --> 00:16:58.480
bear as an example.
Apollo Robbins: Yep.&nbsp;

00:16:58.480 --> 00:17:05.944
Tim Ferriss: So, bear drop off, see&nbsp;
you next week. What exactly happens?&nbsp;

00:17:05.944 --> 00:17:07.040
Apollo Robbins: How do you get a bear —
Tim Ferriss: Like how does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.040 --> 00:17:09.320
the bear make its way to you?
Apollo Robbins: Apparently, now, they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.320 --> 00:17:15.120
a mail order service, bear in a box. Just kidding.
Tim Ferriss: Be careful on the internet, you might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.120 --> 00:17:16.467
get something you don't expect.
Apollo Robbins: Yeah.&nbsp;

00:17:16.467 --> 00:17:19.600
Tim Ferriss: If you try to order that.
Apollo Robbins: Yeah, my address is online. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.600 --> 00:17:24.462
at that time, I had raccoons, which is a&nbsp;
predecessor to getting a bear, as I've learned.&nbsp;

00:17:24.462 --> 00:17:28.440
Tim Ferriss: It's the bear starter kit.
Apollo Robbins: I had worked with the Humane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:35.200
Society as a volunteer from eight. I was an early&nbsp;
entrepreneur from the time I was five. I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.200 --> 00:17:38.000
always doing some kind of hustle, some job.
Tim Ferriss: Was that encouraged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:42.760
did that just emerge sort of out of&nbsp;
your programming out of the box? Is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.760 --> 00:17:45.160
this something other people were doing?&nbsp;
It sounds like you had a family that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.160 --> 00:17:51.160
was sort of active in extracurriculars. But —
Apollo Robbins: Yeah. If you run the definition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.160 --> 00:17:52.904
of extracurriculars, yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Right.&nbsp;

00:17:52.904 --> 00:17:59.440
Apollo Robbins: Well, there's a specific moment.&nbsp;
So, at five, I was at Walmart and I saw a toy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:04.160
rifle that I really wanted. It was a single&nbsp;
bullet, had a little toy bullet inside of it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:09.000
and I thought, I must have this. And my parents&nbsp;
didn't have money to buy it, but they said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:13.800
"If you want to get it, you're going to have to&nbsp;
come back and buy it, but write down this number."&nbsp;

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:16.840
And I wrote down the number, the cost of&nbsp;
what it costs to get that. And they said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:19.600
"It's probably going to be there for this amount&nbsp;
of time, so you've got to figure out how to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.600 --> 00:18:23.840
that much money." And so I had to learn to count&nbsp;
that much money. So I had to learn how to count&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.840 --> 00:18:27.280
the coins. I was finding pennies everywhere,&nbsp;
doing anything I could to try to get jobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.280 --> 00:18:31.400
from everybody. And I got all these pennies&nbsp;
and nickels and dimes, and they took me, my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.400 --> 00:18:35.560
dad walked with me to the bank, and we got these&nbsp;
paper rolls and he had me put everything in there.&nbsp;

00:18:36.280 --> 00:18:40.880
And as we put the paper rolls up, we'd count&nbsp;
up these dollar bills and other things. Walked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:46.080
to the store and I brought all these paper&nbsp;
rolls of coins and paid for that rifle. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.080 --> 00:18:50.040
it was probably about a month's work. And&nbsp;
after that, I thought, how can I do this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:54.120
faster than going around collecting pennies? So&nbsp;
I was like, how else do you make money? Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.120 --> 00:18:59.840
there's a lot of things in the store I want.
And so, from paper routes to creating a little art&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.840 --> 00:19:04.240
business where — I had a knack for drawing when&nbsp;
I was a kid, even though I was disabled a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.240 --> 00:19:08.920
bit. I was early to pick up on 2D perspective,&nbsp;
two-dimensional perspective or three-point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.920 --> 00:19:14.920
perspective. And so it gave me a unique insight,&nbsp;
I think, at that age, that I went back to a back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.920 --> 00:19:18.720
of a comic shop, took down the flyers of all the&nbsp;
artists there that were the best, and said, "Hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.720 --> 00:19:23.280
if I bring you work, will you — instead of giving&nbsp;
me a percentage, will you teach me lessons?"&nbsp;

00:19:23.280 --> 00:19:28.120
And so I started mentoring and finding&nbsp;
mentors very young. And that has persisted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.120 --> 00:19:30.480
with everything I've learned since then.
Tim Ferriss: Okay. We're going to talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.480 --> 00:19:35.747
mentors. How does the bear in a box fit into this?
Apollo Robbins: Okay, back to bear in a box. Yes?&nbsp;

00:19:35.747 --> 00:19:39.480
Tim Ferriss: So you get paid to babysit bears?
Apollo Robbins: No. It was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.480 --> 00:19:42.824
slight ADD tangent. So —
Tim Ferriss: If so, I'm in.&nbsp;

00:19:42.824 --> 00:19:43.417
Apollo Robbins: Yeah, yeah,&nbsp;
yeah. No. It was right —&nbsp;

00:19:43.417 --> 00:19:47.040
Tim Ferriss: What am I doing podcasting?
Apollo Robbins: But I was — along that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.040 --> 00:19:52.480
so I was doing lots of little efforts, like I was&nbsp;
used to working jobs. And I went to the Humane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.480 --> 00:19:56.760
Society and I volunteered for a couple of&nbsp;
weekends to help out. And I saw a puppy get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.760 --> 00:20:01.840
euthanized because it didn't have enough space,&nbsp;
and I was like eight. And that hit me pretty hard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.840 --> 00:20:04.960
because they just didn't have space.
And I was drawing a lot, and I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.960 --> 00:20:08.840
really interested at that time of being&nbsp;
an architect. So I redrew spaces of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.840 --> 00:20:15.280
cage to do triple-stacked cages, and they ended up&nbsp;
saying they would use the idea, so more dogs could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:19.440
stay there. And they added me on to the board of&nbsp;
my local Humane Society when I was like eight.&nbsp;

00:20:20.680 --> 00:20:25.040
Then I said, "I want to help other kind of&nbsp;
animals," because all these orphan animals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.040 --> 00:20:29.600
I studied their habitats, and — I never have said&nbsp;
this stuff out loud, so it really paints a picture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:35.400
of myself that I haven't seen before, but it's&nbsp;
fun. So then raccoons were part of it. They were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:40.520
orphaned and they brought two raccoons to me,&nbsp;
and I raised them and reacclimated them back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.520 --> 00:20:46.000
be released into the wild, and I had a knack for&nbsp;
that of getting animals to successfully re-release&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.000 --> 00:20:48.520
into the wild at an early age.
Tim Ferriss: What do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:52.480
think made you good at that?
Apollo Robbins: When you can't walk the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.480 --> 00:20:57.600
as everyone else, you end up watching everyone&nbsp;
else. And you become a people watcher, and there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:57.600 --> 00:21:03.720
a lot of commonality between animals and people&nbsp;
in those ways of — you start to do a lot of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:08.360
I call perspective shifting, of jumping into their&nbsp;
head to think, "Why would they make that decision?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.360 --> 00:21:12.600
Why would they not do that?" I think that helped&nbsp;
with the animals because I had to figure out, "How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.600 --> 00:21:17.800
do I teach raccoons how to forage? How do I create&nbsp;
little plastic containers with mud and crawfish?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.800 --> 00:21:21.920
And, "What are the things they're going to need&nbsp;
and why are they not adopting that?" "Why would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.920 --> 00:21:26.400
they..." So I was very interested in behaviors of&nbsp;
that at that time. So that's probably part of it.&nbsp;

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:30.240
But then this lady who was mentoring me&nbsp;
on that, her name was Charlie Strothkamp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.240 --> 00:21:34.640
and she was a fascinating lady who'd been a law&nbsp;
enforcement officer that got mauled in a bar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.640 --> 00:21:38.160
fight where she was trying to pull some people&nbsp;
out of bar fight and then sliced open her arm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:44.120
and she had a single arm, but she took care of&nbsp;
bears and buffalo and all sorts of crazy things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.120 --> 00:21:50.000
She's a powerful lady. And she said, "Hey, it's&nbsp;
amazing what you just did with these raccoons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.000 --> 00:21:53.280
Would you like to take care of this young&nbsp;
bear for me for the weekend? It has diapers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.280 --> 00:21:58.467
and it needs to be fed." And it was magical. It&nbsp;
was an awesome little milestone at that time.&nbsp;

00:21:58.467 --> 00:22:02.080
Tim Ferriss: Wow. How old were you?
Apollo Robbins: I was probably nine. Probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.080 --> 00:22:06.840
eight to 10 is when I was doing those things.
Tim Ferriss: So it seems that, to your parents'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.840 --> 00:22:09.864
credit, they were open to you&nbsp;
having a bear in the house?&nbsp;

00:22:09.864 --> 00:22:12.000
Apollo Robbins: Well, not in the house.
Tim Ferriss:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:15.480
So where did the bear in the diaper stay?
Apollo Robbins: I had a habitat that I was using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.480 --> 00:22:19.800
for the raccoons that was square footage is large&nbsp;
enough to let them move and roam that I could —&nbsp;

00:22:19.800 --> 00:22:22.824
Tim Ferriss: Like a shed type of place?&nbsp;
Or was it more of an outdoor enclosure?&nbsp;

00:22:22.824 --> 00:22:25.960
Apollo Robbins: It was an outdoor enclosure that&nbsp;
was chained and fenced in with some options for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:25.960 --> 00:22:29.800
it to go inside that could work for it, but&nbsp;
I had to build a lot of those. My mom had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.800 --> 00:22:32.480
background of farm, so she taught me how to&nbsp;
build a lot of those. I lived in the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.480 --> 00:22:43.720
but we had a strange backyard, strange house.
Tim Ferriss: So let's pick up with the book and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.720 --> 00:22:54.160
the path of magic. So how do you go then from&nbsp;
let's just say broad magic, generalist magic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:54.160 --> 00:23:01.840
to what you have become so well known for? So,&nbsp;
well, first you go to med school and then you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.840 --> 00:23:06.280
figure out your specialty and then you do your&nbsp;
residency and you're like, "Okay. Actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.280 --> 00:23:09.840
I'm not really great at this, but I'm pretty good&nbsp;
at this. So let me zig and zag." Was it that type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.840 --> 00:23:15.944
of process or how did you meander your way? Maybe&nbsp;
it wasn't meandered, maybe it was a direct line.&nbsp;

00:23:15.944 --> 00:23:19.640
Apollo Robbins: I was always full force in&nbsp;
whatever I did. And I think that that's part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.640 --> 00:23:25.040
of it, that sometimes when we're trying to get&nbsp;
to a place and we just feel like we need to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:25.040 --> 00:23:30.400
to that place, we can change the vehicle. We just&nbsp;
know that we're on that journey, and that's often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:30.400 --> 00:23:35.560
what it was. I knew I was getting out of where&nbsp;
I was and that was a big thing for me is getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.560 --> 00:23:38.680
out of the place where I was growing up.
Tim Ferriss: This was going to be my next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:38.680 --> 00:23:46.080
question. So at what point did you have&nbsp;
the conviction that magic or some variant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.080 --> 00:23:52.080
of magic could be your metaphorical&nbsp;
bus ticket out of that place in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.080 --> 00:23:56.320
way that you hoped cartooning would be?
Apollo Robbins: Yeah, which is a fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:56.320 --> 00:24:05.320
combination. So the book was a dense read, and I&nbsp;
think it helped me though, but now when people try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:05.320 --> 00:24:10.400
to learn from magic, they try to learn it without&nbsp;
mentors and from YouTube videos where they don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:14.840
have to deal with people. And so you don't have to&nbsp;
do the perspective taking and then management of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:14.840 --> 00:24:18.840
a person in the same way that when you're first&nbsp;
learning it, you're just learning it to full a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:18.840 --> 00:24:23.160
camera or through a certain angle, and you only&nbsp;
have to do it once. So it's a different thing.&nbsp;

00:24:23.160 --> 00:24:27.400
And at that time, when I was reading it from a&nbsp;
book, I didn't have a mentor. So I was reading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:27.400 --> 00:24:33.200
this dense copy that described how something&nbsp;
was supposed to look magical. I was trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:39.200
transfer that into an idea and doing that in front&nbsp;
of a mirror, and I started to find ways to do it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:43.160
whether they were the way the book intended or&nbsp;
not. But what I had at my disposal, I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:43.160 --> 00:24:49.240
is also because I had been exposed to my brothers&nbsp;
stealing things and other things, it allowed me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:49.240 --> 00:24:56.240
to have a perspective that I didn't have the same&nbsp;
baseline other people did for guilty knowledge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:56.240 --> 00:25:01.880
for an anxiety that happens when you get caught.
And so I was willing to try things that other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:01.880 --> 00:25:07.600
people thought made them shake, it made their skin&nbsp;
shake, make them do — and I was very comfortable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:07.600 --> 00:25:13.760
with doing a lot of unusual things with magic that&nbsp;
really started to shape. For instance, instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:13.760 --> 00:25:18.840
of trying to do what you can call complete vanish&nbsp;
where a coin's going to disappear but rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.840 --> 00:25:22.720
retaining in your hands, you hide it somewhere&nbsp;
in a pocket or somewhere else. I thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:22.720 --> 00:25:27.080
"Why not put it on another person? They won't find&nbsp;
it there." And I'd had that experience. One time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:27.080 --> 00:25:33.600
I was shoplifting early, probably 13, and —
Tim Ferriss: What were your go-to shoplifting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:33.600 --> 00:25:37.504
items? And were you shoplifting to use&nbsp;
them, or to resell them, or something else?&nbsp;

00:25:37.504 --> 00:25:40.400
Apollo Robbins: There was a group,&nbsp;
you call them hobos back in the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:40.400 --> 00:25:45.560
but there was a group of people that lived out&nbsp;
behind some of the grocery stores and they knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:45.560 --> 00:25:49.840
sometimes when I'd run away from home, I'd go stay&nbsp;
with them. And sometimes they would need food,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:49.840 --> 00:25:54.800
or they would need other things, or cigarettes,&nbsp;
or other things like that. And at one time when I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:54.800 --> 00:26:01.200
went in, I stole some cigarettes. I didn't smoke,&nbsp;
but I stole some cigarettes and the guy recognized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:03.760
me and he came up to talk to me.
Tim Ferriss: "He's the kid who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:03.760 --> 00:26:04.920
never buys anything."
Apollo Robbins: Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:04.920 --> 00:26:09.080
he recognized me from, I think, my family, I&nbsp;
lived close by. And he came up to talk to me, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:09.080 --> 00:26:16.000
I didn't want to be found with them. And I think&nbsp;
he saw me do something. So he went to check me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:21.520
and it was an interesting thing just by instinct&nbsp;
that I didn't put them on any pocket on me. I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:21.520 --> 00:26:27.640
left them underneath my arm facing out the back.
So as he's checking my pockets, I reached around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:27.640 --> 00:26:31.360
behind my back with my right hand. I removed&nbsp;
the cigarettes from there and I load them in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:31.360 --> 00:26:38.160
his apron that was on him. So he definitely wasn't&nbsp;
going to look there. And I left and I got away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:38.160 --> 00:26:42.240
but later he's going to find the cigarettes&nbsp;
in his apron. But I think that influenced my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:42.240 --> 00:26:46.560
style of magic too because very quickly&nbsp;
I would take traditional magic effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:46.560 --> 00:26:51.440
and things would be called an impossible location&nbsp;
where something disappears from myself and appears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:51.440 --> 00:26:54.800
somewhere else. I said, "Well, why doesn't it&nbsp;
appear on someone else? What else can I do with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.800 --> 00:26:59.760
that?" So I started developing a lot of that too.
Tim Ferriss: So where did you go after the book?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:59.760 --> 00:27:07.680
You have this initial tome that you're&nbsp;
digesting and shaping and experimenting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:07.680 --> 00:27:15.984
with. Course one. Flip the last page.&nbsp;
You've tested what there is to test.&nbsp;

00:27:15.984 --> 00:27:24.800
Apollo Robbins: I was trying to find a magician&nbsp;
I had seen when I was seven, and there was a guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:24.800 --> 00:27:29.120
that I thought he was a magician, but I didn't&nbsp;
realize he was a magician quite at the time. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:29.120 --> 00:27:37.200
when I was second grade, this guy had come through&nbsp;
my school. And it was just an announcement from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:37.200 --> 00:27:40.080
the principal that called the teacher out of&nbsp;
the room, and there's this guy working on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:40.080 --> 00:27:43.960
window. He was in overalls, and he was working&nbsp;
on the window and he asked one of the kids to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:43.960 --> 00:27:47.200
help measure a rope. And then he cut the rope&nbsp;
and it went back together. It wasn't a show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:47.200 --> 00:27:50.760
it was just these magical things were happening&nbsp;
from this old guy that was fixing the window. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:50.760 --> 00:27:54.800
then everything that came out of his toolbox that&nbsp;
he was trying to use was doing some weird thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:54.800 --> 00:27:57.520
but it wasn't a magic show. It wasn't&nbsp;
presented in that social contract.&nbsp;

00:27:58.480 --> 00:28:04.400
And it hit me over the head. And later I found&nbsp;
that that plastic thumb that I found was a gift to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:04.400 --> 00:28:11.840
my parents from him. So later I went back to try&nbsp;
to find him and found his widow, and she told me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:11.840 --> 00:28:16.520
about this magic club that was in my hometown.
And when I showed up, they said, "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:16.520 --> 00:28:19.680
you're new to magic." And I started doing&nbsp;
some things and they said, "Why can you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:19.680 --> 00:28:25.040
do that?" And there's two things I could do.&nbsp;
I could manipulate coins, make them appear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:25.040 --> 00:28:31.920
disappear, small objects like that. And I could&nbsp;
do what's called equivoque, an influence of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:31.920 --> 00:28:36.920
series of choices that I learned early on when&nbsp;
I was eight from some book, but I had used it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:36.920 --> 00:28:41.400
over the years and I could do that, which is&nbsp;
a category of mentalism that is jazz-based.&nbsp;

00:28:41.400 --> 00:28:44.960
Tim Ferriss: Did you say jazz?
Apollo Robbins: Yeah. My whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:44.960 --> 00:28:48.680
style is jazz-based, which is —&nbsp;
and that's an important thing.&nbsp;

00:28:48.680 --> 00:28:53.840
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Please say more. And equivoque,&nbsp;
just so I understand what that means, I'm trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:53.840 --> 00:28:59.480
to dissect the etymology, but I'm going to get&nbsp;
stuck in the weeds there. You are, in very subtle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:59.480 --> 00:29:04.584
ways, directing someone's choice, even though&nbsp;
they think they have free will and selection?&nbsp;

00:29:04.584 --> 00:29:06.960
Apollo Robbins: You give the illusion of agency.
Tim Ferriss: I see.&nbsp;

00:29:06.960 --> 00:29:10.800
Apollo Robbins: You're allowing them to play a&nbsp;
game of darts, but you're painting the bullseye&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:10.800 --> 00:29:13.384
on the wall after the dart has landed.
Tim Ferriss: I see.&nbsp;

00:29:13.384 --> 00:29:16.040
Apollo Robbins: And yet they don't realize&nbsp;
that they feel with conviction that that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:16.040 --> 00:29:20.560
was all choices that they made that they&nbsp;
navigated to. And that takes a fluidity of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:20.560 --> 00:29:25.200
thought. There's little feedback loops that&nbsp;
you blend in the segues and people don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:25.200 --> 00:29:33.720
notice those and the way you use your words.
Tim Ferriss: Please correct me if this is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:33.720 --> 00:29:39.360
lazy or inaccurate description, but for people&nbsp;
who don't know the term mentalism, one way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:39.360 --> 00:29:46.680
that could manifest is someone demonstrating&nbsp;
mind reading or something along those lines?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:46.680 --> 00:29:49.720
What is the right way to think of mentalism?
Apollo Robbins: I think there's a variety of ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:49.720 --> 00:29:54.800
but if you think about card magic, when you see&nbsp;
magician pick a card, where did that come from?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:54.800 --> 00:30:00.560
It came from at one point some card mechanics or&nbsp;
card sharps that cheated at cards, some of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:00.560 --> 00:30:06.120
filtered across and made it into the entertainment&nbsp;
world. And there's this other category of a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:06.120 --> 00:30:11.080
of those from thieves and things factored into&nbsp;
magic. There's an interesting genealogy there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:11.080 --> 00:30:18.560
I think. But mentalism came from psychics and&nbsp;
mediums. And that category was this impersonation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:18.560 --> 00:30:23.840
that I have some type of exceptional ability&nbsp;
to read minds, influence, predict the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:23.840 --> 00:30:30.360
talk to the dead, whatever it might be. The&nbsp;
different label is that, as a camp, mentalists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:30.360 --> 00:30:35.560
usually will not claim that they have real powers.&nbsp;
They'll say that they're doing it under the flag&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:35.560 --> 00:30:44.120
of entertainment versus psychics and mediums who&nbsp;
often use the same tools will sometimes convey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:44.120 --> 00:30:46.104
that they have those God-given powers.
Tim Ferriss: Some superpowers.&nbsp;

00:30:46.104 --> 00:30:47.480
Apollo Robbins: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Got it. Okay. So you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:47.480 --> 00:30:52.307
could do the coin manipulation.
Apollo Robbins: Yes.&nbsp;

00:30:52.307 --> 00:30:57.640
Tim Ferriss: And you also had some mentalism game&nbsp;
and they were like — "Wait a second, you just said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:57.640 --> 00:31:01.640
you were new to all this. So how are you able&nbsp;
to do these things?" And you gave the specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:01.640 --> 00:31:07.840
example of the equivoque. And then what happened?
Apollo Robbins: I told them I'd read these books&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:07.840 --> 00:31:11.907
and I'd learned it on my own. And they were&nbsp;
surprised by that and they said, "Well..."&nbsp;

00:31:11.907 --> 00:31:14.440
Tim Ferriss: That must've felt good.
Apollo Robbins: Yeah, it was nice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:14.440 --> 00:31:19.760
And I also learned early there that even though I&nbsp;
learned some things wrong by their interpretation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:19.760 --> 00:31:22.760
it created a new thing. And they said, "Well,&nbsp;
that wasn't what they meant." I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:22.760 --> 00:31:26.800
"Oh, that's great. So what did they mean?"
Tim Ferriss: Now, is that what you mean by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:26.800 --> 00:31:31.744
jazz-influenced? If you make a mistake,&nbsp;
do it twice and then it's jazz or —&nbsp;

00:31:31.744 --> 00:31:37.240
Apollo Robbins: No, it's a different thing.&nbsp;
So if you think of a lot of performance magic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:37.240 --> 00:31:44.320
it is very sheet music-based, very classical. It's&nbsp;
very structured. There's a lot of geometry and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:44.320 --> 00:31:50.880
sight lines and the calculations of that. There's&nbsp;
very precise scripting of what's going on. My&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:50.880 --> 00:31:57.600
style has always been very jazz-based to a fault&nbsp;
in many ways, but that I would feel constricted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:57.600 --> 00:32:03.760
by any script. So for me, as soon as I had an&nbsp;
idea, my landscape was to try it with an audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:03.760 --> 00:32:07.440
immediately before I'd even try tested it out.
Tim Ferriss: Pick up a turtle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:07.440 --> 00:32:10.800
walk in front of that audience, figure it out.
Apollo Robbins: Well, yeah. Jump off the cliff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:10.800 --> 00:32:13.960
and build a plane on the way down. But yes, the&nbsp;
turtle is a great metaphor for that. I mean, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:13.960 --> 00:32:20.520
first time I did a show with my wife, and&nbsp;
initially we had this separate tension to go down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:20.520 --> 00:32:25.160
but we were walking to go on stage and there&nbsp;
was a certain skill set we learned with each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:25.160 --> 00:32:29.720
other. And she said, "But what's the structure&nbsp;
for the show?" I said, "I don't know. I haven't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:29.720 --> 00:32:34.144
met the audience yet." And I didn't —
Tim Ferriss: And she was like, "Ahhh."&nbsp;

00:32:34.144 --> 00:32:37.200
Apollo Robbins: Which is this collision of two&nbsp;
different mindsets, which she's changed me for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:37.200 --> 00:32:40.880
better. At that time, I didn't want to start until&nbsp;
I knew — until I met the audience. It's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:40.880 --> 00:32:44.400
"Let's create this together," because I thought&nbsp;
there was a value in creating. And to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:44.400 --> 00:32:48.360
early on in magic, that's what I was trying to&nbsp;
do too because all the books said I had to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:48.360 --> 00:32:52.320
this in phase one, then phase two, then phase&nbsp;
three. It's like, I get that, and that's useful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:52.320 --> 00:32:56.240
for some things, but also I want the organic&nbsp;
feeling that this person feels like they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:56.240 --> 00:33:01.093
having this experience and it's not happening to&nbsp;
anybody else. This only happened in this moment.&nbsp;

00:33:01.093 --> 00:33:04.320
Tim Ferriss: All right. So did that&nbsp;
magic club figure into your skill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:04.320 --> 00:33:09.984
development? Was it a launching pad?&nbsp;
Where did you end up going from there?&nbsp;

00:33:09.984 --> 00:33:14.664
Apollo Robbins: They offered me books, more books.
Tim Ferriss: So you like books?&nbsp;

00:33:14.664 --> 00:33:18.480
Apollo Robbins: Yeah. And they said, "Well, if you&nbsp;
could get through that book that you first read,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:18.480 --> 00:33:22.520
then why don't you take these other books&nbsp;
and learn some of those things?" Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:22.520 --> 00:33:25.640
there's this great quote, I don't know who&nbsp;
it's attributed to, but "When we buy books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:25.640 --> 00:33:32.680
we think we're buying the time to read them."
Tim Ferriss: As my unread stack would agree.&nbsp;

00:33:32.680 --> 00:33:35.400
Apollo Robbins: And I think that that's&nbsp;
what they were suffering from at that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:35.400 --> 00:33:39.387
time is that you have this energy, you&nbsp;
read my books, and teach it back to me.&nbsp;

00:33:39.387 --> 00:33:40.960
Tim Ferriss: Genius.
Apollo Robbins: Yeah. So they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:40.960 --> 00:33:45.582
did that to me and that was a really cool thing.&nbsp;
There was a couple of people that influenced my —&nbsp;

00:33:45.582 --> 00:33:49.560
Tim Ferriss: It's a magic trick of its own type.
Apollo Robbins: It is, yes. But Ben was a mentor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:49.560 --> 00:33:54.360
He wasn't with the club, but Ben did some things.&nbsp;
He did one thing. There was a magician I really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:54.360 --> 00:34:00.800
admired who helped me on the business side named&nbsp;
Mark Sparks, who was a traveling performer who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:00.800 --> 00:34:07.280
was brilliant. And I'd seen him make a coin fly up&nbsp;
from one hand to another, and anything I could try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:07.280 --> 00:34:11.680
to excuse or sleight of hand didn't make sense of&nbsp;
why that could happen. And other books I'd read,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:11.680 --> 00:34:15.800
it didn't make sense of why that could happen.&nbsp;
So I went back to Ben Stone in that shop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:15.800 --> 00:34:22.000
we called Mr. E's Magic Shop. I said, "Ben, what&nbsp;
is with this coin?" "I don't know. I think it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:22.000 --> 00:34:25.920
this. Here's my hypothesis one, two, three."
And he goes, "Ah. Well, that's obviously Dariel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:25.920 --> 00:34:30.440
Fitzkee." I said, "Fitzkee?" And I said, "I don't&nbsp;
know, that's not on my radar." And he says, "Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:30.440 --> 00:34:33.920
Fitzkee wrote three books. It's a trilogy.&nbsp;
Go read that." And so I picked up one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:33.920 --> 00:34:39.160
books, and it's called Magic by Misdirection&nbsp;
on the psychology of magic. I read it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:39.160 --> 00:34:43.000
and it was a hard read. And I came back and I&nbsp;
said, "Ben, it wasn't in there." And he says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:43.000 --> 00:34:47.880
"Well, he wrote two other books, The Trick Brain&nbsp;
and Showmanship for Magicians." So I read all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:47.880 --> 00:34:51.720
those. I came back and I said, "It wasn't in&nbsp;
any of those." He goes, "I must be wrong."&nbsp;

00:34:51.720 --> 00:34:56.520
And Ben tricked me again. He tricked me to&nbsp;
use my desire to learn how to make that coin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:56.520 --> 00:35:02.480
fly up to read three books that have influenced&nbsp;
me to this day of a very structured, analytical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:02.480 --> 00:35:08.520
approach for performance that I needed, but also&nbsp;
for the psychology of magic and understanding how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:08.520 --> 00:35:13.680
an illusion is constructed in the audience's mind.&nbsp;
And I needed that at 15 because I wasn't thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:13.680 --> 00:35:19.880
about that. So that was the biggest thing, I would&nbsp;
say. And there was many other people that — from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:19.880 --> 00:35:25.760
that time period, a gentleman named Ed Dillard,&nbsp;
who was a year-round Santa Claus who did amazing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:25.760 --> 00:35:29.867
things. He would go into a mall and he created&nbsp;
experiences. That was a great thing because —&nbsp;

00:35:29.867 --> 00:35:33.560
Tim Ferriss: He was a year-round Santa Claus?
Apollo Robbins: Yes, he lived it all year. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:33.560 --> 00:35:39.440
anybody who saw him — but when you'd see him in&nbsp;
a mall — you see people go up and a kid sits on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:39.440 --> 00:35:44.160
Santa Claus. But what would be effective with him&nbsp;
is a 12-year old would say "Hi," at Santa Claus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:44.160 --> 00:35:48.360
and he says, "It's good to see you, Nick. So&nbsp;
what's been happening this year, Nick? It sounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:48.360 --> 00:35:51.480
like you've had some problems with your friend.&nbsp;
We want to talk about it." And he would say these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:51.480 --> 00:35:58.240
things and just know so much about someone. And&nbsp;
he was mentalist as well. And not to also throw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:58.240 --> 00:36:02.200
off that he had an inner ear microphone in his&nbsp;
ear and the parents had the other side of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:02.200 --> 00:36:06.080
but he had this great book of Latin that was&nbsp;
Santa's book, and it was all carved out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:06.080 --> 00:36:09.800
leather with this beautiful book, and he would&nbsp;
tell anybody about anything about themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:09.800 --> 00:36:14.200
but through the auspices of Santa Claus.
And I just loved this beautiful approach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:14.200 --> 00:36:19.200
that it wasn't so much of what I saw magicians&nbsp;
initially getting in a magic for was to fill a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:19.200 --> 00:36:22.600
hole in themselves. They were hiding behind&nbsp;
what they're doing, they're trying to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:22.600 --> 00:36:28.040
this validation from somewhere, and they needed&nbsp;
attention. And that wasn't the driver for me. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:28.040 --> 00:36:33.120
was trying to figure out, "Is there something here&nbsp;
I can create sparks in people, an experience like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:33.120 --> 00:36:39.160
that guy did in my second grade when it came in&nbsp;
as repairing the window or the Santa Claus?" So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:39.160 --> 00:36:44.520
I think it started to shape that I had one skill&nbsp;
set, which, outside of magic, I could steal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:44.520 --> 00:36:51.360
things that I could blend in. And there was a few&nbsp;
factors also. I stage a little bit martial arts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:51.360 --> 00:36:54.920
I had some understanding of animation.&nbsp;
And all of them kind of settled together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:54.920 --> 00:36:59.264
to create a style that I developed in that shape.
Tim Ferriss: Is the animation and form the style?&nbsp;

00:36:59.264 --> 00:37:02.880
Apollo Robbins: Animation, there was a&nbsp;
great book called The Illusion of Life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:02.880 --> 00:37:09.600
by Disney's — what do you call them? His nine&nbsp;
men. But it talks about the illusion of life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:10.560 --> 00:37:16.000
in just frame by frame when you're hand drawing,&nbsp;
how to create life motion. And one of those is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:16.000 --> 00:37:21.280
secondary actions and what they call slowing in&nbsp;
and slowing out, something moving at the top of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:21.280 --> 00:37:25.600
curve, how it will have more frames versus on the&nbsp;
bottom of a curve. And I started realizing there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:25.600 --> 00:37:30.200
overlaps in relationships of that to Aikido. And&nbsp;
when you study Aikido, there are certain half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:30.200 --> 00:37:34.760
moves and half circles. And so when I started&nbsp;
blending those together with sleight of hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:34.760 --> 00:37:40.480
I realized that I could lock people in a&nbsp;
dance. When you're doing a cross body lead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:40.480 --> 00:37:44.520
how does somebody know that you have a lead and&nbsp;
how do you transfer that with your hands? So by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:44.520 --> 00:37:49.240
putting those together, I started to see that I&nbsp;
could manipulate a person's body and tell them a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:49.240 --> 00:37:53.640
story that they wouldn't register consciously, and&nbsp;
I could lock up their body without them knowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:53.640 --> 00:37:55.760
that I had locked up the mechanics.
Tim Ferriss: Lock up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:55.760 --> 00:37:59.120
meaning like gain control, hijack?
Apollo Robbins: Yeah. Put them in a frame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:59.120 --> 00:38:02.880
as you would say, on the street. So I could put&nbsp;
them in a frame that I didn't need other people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:02.880 --> 00:38:08.040
a team of thieves to help me. I could do it by&nbsp;
myself and I could use certain things that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:08.040 --> 00:38:11.880
draw. I noticed from animation because of that,&nbsp;
there were certain motions that drew attention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:11.880 --> 00:38:17.320
and that later became a thing, too.
Tim Ferriss: So a lot of people try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:17.320 --> 00:38:25.800
magic in some form or another. Very few people&nbsp;
make it to the point where they are performing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:25.800 --> 00:38:32.480
giving TED talks, getting profiled by The New&nbsp;
Yorker, et cetera. Very few. What were some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:32.480 --> 00:38:39.480
most important inflection points, say, picking up&nbsp;
where we left off and after reading these books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:39.480 --> 00:38:46.200
after having these various inputs, I guess maybe&nbsp;
it could have been during these various inputs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:46.200 --> 00:38:54.600
like the martial arts and the jazz and the dance&nbsp;
and so on. What were some of the critical moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:54.600 --> 00:38:58.600
let's say? Could be decisions, could&nbsp;
be interactions, could be people you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:58.600 --> 00:39:07.680
meet that helped to set their trajectory.
Apollo Robbins: I'm so fortunate to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:07.680 --> 00:39:14.520
had some incredible people influence me along the&nbsp;
way, and I've surfed through my career from person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:14.520 --> 00:39:21.760
to person that's made an impact on me. But back&nbsp;
at that time, there's one I'd say that makes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:21.760 --> 00:39:26.560
most impact, which is my wife. I'll circle back&nbsp;
to her because she's probably the biggest. But at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:26.560 --> 00:39:33.800
that time, when I was in Las Vegas, I was married&nbsp;
when I was young. And so it was my first wife.&nbsp;

00:39:33.800 --> 00:39:38.600
Tim Ferriss: When did you first get married?
Apollo Robbins: I was 18. So I was married.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:38.600 --> 00:39:44.480
I had a son. Now I have two kids. I have a son&nbsp;
who's 30 and a daughter who's six. So fascinating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:44.480 --> 00:39:49.960
two different worlds. But at that time, I&nbsp;
had to provide for my family. So it amped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:49.960 --> 00:39:54.880
up that entrepreneurship that I think made it&nbsp;
a very serious thing that I had to find out if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:54.880 --> 00:40:00.440
I'm going to do this — I was in a juxtaposition&nbsp;
between two worlds. I had given up art because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:00.440 --> 00:40:06.040
this incident with my father, but in school and&nbsp;
high school, I had some teachers recognize that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:06.040 --> 00:40:11.120
I shouldn't, and they pushed me toward design&nbsp;
and illustration and computer graphics. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:11.120 --> 00:40:16.520
had two careers that I could do simultaneously,&nbsp;
the design aspect of that, and then moonlighting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:16.520 --> 00:40:21.040
with the magic style and comedy clubs and&nbsp;
things. So I was doing both of those in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:21.040 --> 00:40:26.840
high school. But then I was transitioning into&nbsp;
doing that full-time by the time both of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:26.840 --> 00:40:33.080
simultaneously as I graduated high school.
And there was a gentleman who was a touring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:33.080 --> 00:40:37.360
magician from Las Vegas, came through&nbsp;
town and he saw me performing and he said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:37.360 --> 00:40:41.160
"You should go to Vegas." And I said, "Yeah,&nbsp;
that's what everybody says." And he says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:41.160 --> 00:40:44.280
"Come visit me." I said, "I've never flown on a&nbsp;
plane before. I don't even know what it's like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:44.280 --> 00:40:49.680
outside of Missouri." And he says, "Come."
And I flew to Vegas to visit this guy. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:49.680 --> 00:40:55.280
name was Daryl Martinez. They called him The&nbsp;
Magician's Magician. And he picked me up at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:55.280 --> 00:40:59.600
the airport. He drove me straight downtown into a&nbsp;
gambling spot. And he was a gambler. He showed me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:59.600 --> 00:41:05.400
that world and he said, "Vegas has everything to&nbsp;
offer, but it will give you nothing." Which was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:05.400 --> 00:41:09.080
perfect for me because I love working for it.
And I formed a model real quick. I looked at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:09.080 --> 00:41:11.840
all the magicians that were around me, and I&nbsp;
recognized that they blew me away. They had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:11.840 --> 00:41:15.960
exceptional skills, things I didn't know were&nbsp;
possible, but they didn't have everything. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:15.960 --> 00:41:21.360
either had high skill, which I put underneath&nbsp;
the talent. They had low business sense maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:21.360 --> 00:41:28.400
or their ambition to take that effort to the&nbsp;
next place. And the combination of those were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:28.400 --> 00:41:32.600
atrophied in different areas. And I felt like&nbsp;
even if I don't have the same skill level, I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:32.600 --> 00:41:38.320
bring some of that back or find other angles.
And so I decided to move out to Las Vegas and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:38.320 --> 00:41:45.520
to try to go after that. I'm jumping through a&nbsp;
timeline real quick, but I was initially working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:45.520 --> 00:41:52.560
at doing a couple of design jobs. And my second&nbsp;
one was for a series of slot machine design,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:52.560 --> 00:41:57.680
designing the animation sequences for slot&nbsp;
machines and doing all the illustrations for that.&nbsp;

00:41:57.680 --> 00:42:03.080
And I was doing freelance artwork for a hypnotist&nbsp;
named Justin Tranz. And I was designing all this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:03.080 --> 00:42:07.880
stuff and he said, "Why are you here?" He says,&nbsp;
"For a couple years you've been in this little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:07.880 --> 00:42:11.520
place. You're doing freelance artwork for me." He&nbsp;
said, "But your performing capability," he says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:11.520 --> 00:42:16.480
"you could just do something else." And I&nbsp;
said, "What's a good reason? Give me one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:16.480 --> 00:42:21.040
good reason." He says, "Wouldn't it be amazing&nbsp;
story sometime later in your life if people said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:21.040 --> 00:42:25.280
'Why did you quit your job as an artist and&nbsp;
become a magician or performer full time?' And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:25.280 --> 00:42:30.280
it was because a hypnotist told you during&nbsp;
a lunch break." I thought that was a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:30.280 --> 00:42:36.142
idea. So I went back into my job and I gave&nbsp;
my notice that day. And that was a Friday.&nbsp;

00:42:36.142 --> 00:42:39.400
Tim Ferriss: Wow, what a gift. Well, it depends on&nbsp;
how that's — I mean, I know you're sitting here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:39.400 --> 00:42:43.784
so I'm not sure the intervening experiences&nbsp;
looked like, but it seems like a gift.&nbsp;

00:42:43.784 --> 00:42:48.360
Apollo Robbins: That was a big point because&nbsp;
that was a jumping point. And it had a context&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:48.360 --> 00:42:51.760
in that I was a senior designer at that&nbsp;
time in the company I was working for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:51.760 --> 00:42:57.520
and the reason why I didn't say their name, they&nbsp;
had led off a large production team that supported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:57.520 --> 00:43:01.747
the graphics I did. I'd hand my designs to them,&nbsp;
they would port them across to bigger things.&nbsp;

00:43:01.747 --> 00:43:03.720
Tim Ferriss: They'd let them go.
Apollo Robbins: They found a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:03.720 --> 00:43:07.760
to get around their contract. So they'd&nbsp;
outsource them to another company and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:07.760 --> 00:43:09.800
then let that company fire them.
Tim Ferriss: Okay, so they wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:09.800 --> 00:43:12.400
owe any severance or they'd dodge something?
Apollo Robbins: Yes, they'd dodge all their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:12.400 --> 00:43:19.600
severance. And that made an impact on me. So they&nbsp;
had just given me a bonus. They'd just given me a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:19.600 --> 00:43:24.520
raise. That was the context at that time that&nbsp;
he said that, "Well, why are you here?" And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:24.520 --> 00:43:28.680
it wasn't about the money for me. And it was that&nbsp;
moment of, "Yeah, what would happen if I did this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:28.680 --> 00:43:32.480
thing? I don't know how, but let me just clean&nbsp;
this slate on this thing and see what happens."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:32.480 --> 00:43:37.720
And coincidences, I still believe, happened.&nbsp;
And that weekend, a friend of mine said, "Hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:37.720 --> 00:43:42.680
I'm going on tour for a couple of weeks and&nbsp;
there's a show called Caesars Magical Empire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:42.680 --> 00:43:45.920
and I was wondering if you could come fill me&nbsp;
in. Fill in for me that for those two weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:46.560 --> 00:43:51.587
starting on Tuesday." I said, "Perfect timing.&nbsp;
That sounds great. I'll give that a shot."&nbsp;

00:43:51.587 --> 00:43:54.520
Tim Ferriss: What did you have ready&nbsp;
at that point? Filling in, does that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:54.520 --> 00:43:59.200
mean you have a 30-minute set, a 15-minute&nbsp;
set? I'm not sure if set is the right term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:59.200 --> 00:44:05.400
60-minute set? I think of comedians working&nbsp;
on standup material and to get a polished hour&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:05.400 --> 00:44:07.947
could take years for something, right?
Apollo Robbins: Yeah, absolutely.&nbsp;

00:44:07.947 --> 00:44:12.840
Tim Ferriss: So what did you have when&nbsp;
this opportunity falls from the sky? "Hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:12.840 --> 00:44:17.200
could you fill in within the next week?"
Apollo Robbins: I'd been doing it professionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:17.200 --> 00:44:22.000
at the same time. So even back in my hometown,&nbsp;
I had contracts with — I did restaurants, I did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:22.000 --> 00:44:29.160
nightclubs. I had 15 different contracts per week.
Tim Ferriss: So you had things you knew had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:29.160 --> 00:44:31.680
already worked so you knew that&nbsp;
you could string them together.&nbsp;

00:44:31.680 --> 00:44:35.520
Apollo Robbins: And I knew I could make&nbsp;
an impact. And the big skill I had that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:35.520 --> 00:44:38.840
was unique when I came to Vegas, I decided,&nbsp;
"Let's just drop the other stuff and just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:38.840 --> 00:44:42.680
focus on the stealing." Because at that point,&nbsp;
there was one other guy who was really good, two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:42.680 --> 00:44:47.400
other guys that were in that space. One&nbsp;
of them had passed away. He was a friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:47.400 --> 00:44:52.747
and he worked at that place. So it was a good&nbsp;
angle for me to explore further in more depth.&nbsp;

00:44:52.747 --> 00:44:55.360
Tim Ferriss: To differentiate yourself.
Apollo Robbins: Yeah, as a brand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:55.360 --> 00:44:59.160
I'll give you — just paint a picture of the —&nbsp;
it's a fascinating thing. My first show I ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:59.160 --> 00:45:06.240
did in Las Vegas, separate from Caesars, it was&nbsp;
just a corporate event for a large corporation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:06.240 --> 00:45:09.360
and it was a red carpet event. They had&nbsp;
limousines pulling in. It was very different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:09.360 --> 00:45:14.920
for a Missouri boy, when I first moved out&nbsp;
there. And these agents, they said, "So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:14.920 --> 00:45:19.280
all right, go steal things from people,"&nbsp;
I guess. And they gave me hundred-dollar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:19.280 --> 00:45:24.160
poker chips to give to people as gifts. And&nbsp;
I went up to this guy, and the agent was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:24.160 --> 00:45:25.640
watching. And the first time I tried to steal —
Tim Ferriss: When you say agent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:25.640 --> 00:45:28.280
this is someone who works at the casino?
Apollo Robbins: These are agents that hire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:28.280 --> 00:45:30.024
for entertainment in Las Vegas.
Tim Ferriss: I see.&nbsp;

00:45:30.024 --> 00:45:31.920
Apollo Robbins: And they were&nbsp;
watching and they were trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:31.920 --> 00:45:35.560
vet if I was a good fit. And the first guy —
Tim Ferriss: This is like your audition gig.&nbsp;

00:45:35.560 --> 00:45:39.560
Apollo Robbins: It was kind of, yeah, if they're&nbsp;
going to hire me back again. And so I walk up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:39.560 --> 00:45:43.360
somebody that was important. They brought&nbsp;
this big VIP up to me right away. And as I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:43.360 --> 00:45:47.960
go to steal his watch, his watchband was very&nbsp;
worn out. And it snapped in two pieces while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:47.960 --> 00:45:57.320
I was stealing it. And I could feel my ears&nbsp;
turning red. And I just was running through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:57.320 --> 00:46:02.280
the gamut. "Do I give this back to him?&nbsp;
Which is more important?" Because I got a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:02.280 --> 00:46:06.400
lot of pressure at that moment. And so I was&nbsp;
performing with him and another gentleman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:06.400 --> 00:46:12.040
and then I said, "Oh, and here's your watch."&nbsp;
And he's shocked that I have his watch. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:12.040 --> 00:46:15.240
before he even processed, I pulled out the&nbsp;
other piece of his watch and gave him the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:15.240 --> 00:46:20.240
second piece of it, which was a leather band.
And I said, "See that lady over there?" He goes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:20.240 --> 00:46:23.320
"That lady?" I said, "Yeah. See her watch?"&nbsp;
I said," I'm going to let you just see it a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:23.320 --> 00:46:26.240
second time. This time I'm going to steal her&nbsp;
watch, and I'm going to try to do it without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:26.240 --> 00:46:29.360
breaking it." Now that's a lot harder, but I'm&nbsp;
going to try to see if it's possible." He goes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:29.360 --> 00:46:32.760
"No, you can't do that." And I was gambling&nbsp;
on the idea that he didn't need the money. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:32.760 --> 00:46:36.400
probably knew that he needed to replace this band,&nbsp;
but I'm reframing it that, "Hey, that's a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:36.400 --> 00:46:40.160
another level. You guys want to try it? And let's&nbsp;
move past this idea that I just broke his watch."&nbsp;

00:46:42.480 --> 00:46:45.000
Tim Ferriss: Yes. Hard to script for.
Apollo Robbins: Yeah. And that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:45.000 --> 00:46:49.640
constantly what was required from me.
Tim Ferriss: "I didn't take cigarettes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:49.640 --> 00:46:53.560
You took cigarettes in the apron."
Apollo Robbins: Yes. So my career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:53.560 --> 00:46:59.040
has been majority of those, right? And when my&nbsp;
first day after I had left my job as a designer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:59.040 --> 00:47:03.400
I come over to work at Caesars Palace. I was&nbsp;
accused of stealing this lady's rings. I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:03.400 --> 00:47:07.800
doing a performance in front of a group. And&nbsp;
she said, "My rings!" And her husband said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:07.800 --> 00:47:12.560
"Where'd they go?" And she says, "Somebody stole&nbsp;
them." I hadn't even touched her. And I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:12.560 --> 00:47:15.920
"What did you have?" And she goes, "I&nbsp;
had diamonds and rubies and emeralds (oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:15.920 --> 00:47:21.160
my)!" And I just said, "Oh, I must've sent them as&nbsp;
a child support payment." I just turned it in as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:21.160 --> 00:47:24.824
joke. And then the husband really got mad.
Tim Ferriss: Didn't like it.&nbsp;

00:47:24.824 --> 00:47:29.040
Apollo Robbins: No. And so once everybody cleared&nbsp;
out, they came at me, come up to me, came at me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:29.040 --> 00:47:34.120
too. And he said, "Give us the rings." I said,&nbsp;
"Do you really believe that I have your rings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:34.120 --> 00:47:38.840
or are you joking?" And he says, "No, give us the&nbsp;
rings." And he says, "We're going to the police."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:38.840 --> 00:47:43.160
And they started to leave. And I said, "Stop.&nbsp;
Don't walk away." And he goes, "What?" I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:43.160 --> 00:47:46.560
"If you walk away right now, you'll never know&nbsp;
if I have your rings because I could hide them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:46.560 --> 00:47:50.680
anywhere. But I want you to know that I don't.&nbsp;
So I'm going to walk in front of you and I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:50.680 --> 00:47:54.160
going to keep my hands out to the side. You watch&nbsp;
me, we're going to walk to the police together."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:54.160 --> 00:47:58.000
So we walked out in the casino, we went to&nbsp;
security, and I got strip searched. And his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:58.000 --> 00:48:02.960
wife went up. She was very dramatic, which to me&nbsp;
conveyed that she was sincere about her belief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:02.960 --> 00:48:08.560
that she really believed that that happened. So&nbsp;
then she up in her room found her ring sitting on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:08.560 --> 00:48:12.224
a sink as she'd been washing her hands.
Tim Ferriss: Did you get an apology?&nbsp;

00:48:12.224 --> 00:48:14.424
Apollo Robbins: I did.
Tim Ferriss: That's good.&nbsp;

00:48:14.424 --> 00:48:19.400
Apollo Robbins: But I think it may also helped.&nbsp;
It wasn't just an apology. It helped me secure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:19.400 --> 00:48:23.800
a job there beyond filling in for a friend.&nbsp;
I got it for five years. So that five years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:23.800 --> 00:48:28.240
came from how I handled that situation. And&nbsp;
over that time, I had people grab my throat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:28.240 --> 00:48:32.600
pull out a gun one time, pull out knives. I had —
Tim Ferriss: Hold on a second. This is at shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:32.600 --> 00:48:35.680
right after a show. Did it happen —
Apollo Robbins: During shows all over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:35.680 --> 00:48:38.907
the place. I've had every variation&nbsp;
of that happen. And that's —&nbsp;

00:48:38.907 --> 00:48:42.584
Tim Ferriss: Because they think you have stolen&nbsp;
something or they're upset that you duped them?&nbsp;

00:48:42.584 --> 00:48:47.560
Apollo Robbins: Or maybe I have, yes. But it&nbsp;
was in a social context of entertainment, right?&nbsp;

00:48:47.560 --> 00:48:49.400
Tim Ferriss: Right, right.
Apollo Robbins: Yeah. But still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:49.400 --> 00:48:53.160
that's a different thing than if you mess up on&nbsp;
a magic trick. If you mess up on a magic trick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:53.160 --> 00:48:57.640
they caught you, "Look, I understand how your&nbsp;
trick works." In the space I was playing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:57.640 --> 00:49:03.400
it was accusations, law enforcement, lawsuits,&nbsp;
and I was constantly doing with that. I learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:03.400 --> 00:49:07.160
certain rules. Don't steal from someone who's&nbsp;
been drinking. Don't steal from someone who has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:07.160 --> 00:49:10.880
Alzheimer's or any potential for that because&nbsp;
that's a really hard one to fight your way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:10.880 --> 00:49:15.720
out of if they believe they had something they&nbsp;
didn't. And I started making my own set of rules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:15.720 --> 00:49:20.320
and it changed one big thing in my style, which&nbsp;
was, all right, I'm going to treat this like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:20.320 --> 00:49:22.584
vampire. I'm going to tell them —
Tim Ferriss: Like a vampire.&nbsp;

00:49:22.584 --> 00:49:25.520
Apollo Robbins: Vampires had to be introduced&nbsp;
into the household, across the threshold.&nbsp;

00:49:25.520 --> 00:49:26.984
Tim Ferriss: You're right,&nbsp;
they need to be invited.&nbsp;

00:49:26.984 --> 00:49:29.960
Apollo Robbins: So I'd say, "In three&nbsp;
minutes I'm going to be wearing your watch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:29.960 --> 00:49:35.320
try to catch me." And if they say "Yes," but&nbsp;
that was the first time I had ever seen that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:35.320 --> 00:49:38.400
happen. When I had studied the history of&nbsp;
theatrical pickpocketing and everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:38.400 --> 00:49:42.960
there wasn't that open challenge that, "Beware,&nbsp;
this is about to happen. If you engage, that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:42.960 --> 00:49:46.440
the game we're going to play. Are you ready?"
But it now meant a whole different thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:46.440 --> 00:49:52.240
but it came from all those concerns and lawsuits&nbsp;
and everything, I'll say, that almost happened.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:52.240 --> 00:49:58.520
Never landed. But now I developed this style that&nbsp;
was a very different style, very potent style,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:58.520 --> 00:50:03.200
but instead of surprise, it was suspense.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah, exactly. It was suspense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:03.200 --> 00:50:05.947
It was also a preemptive strategy.
Apollo Robbins: Yes.&nbsp;

00:50:05.947 --> 00:50:10.464
Tim Ferriss: Right. So you would have&nbsp;
less need to be good at reacting.&nbsp;

00:50:10.464 --> 00:50:11.600
Apollo Robbins: Yes.
Tim Ferriss: Because you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:11.600 --> 00:50:17.040
setting the ground rules and getting&nbsp;
invited as a vampire. At what point then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:17.040 --> 00:50:22.104
you said five years, is it within that span that&nbsp;
Jimmy Carter and the Secret Service show up?&nbsp;

00:50:22.104 --> 00:50:28.480
Apollo Robbins: Yes. So at that time, I had been&nbsp;
there — and I'll give you more context. So when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:28.480 --> 00:50:35.960
I was performing, it was a specific role at the&nbsp;
beginning of the show. 24 people would go into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:35.960 --> 00:50:41.000
a certain area and they'd be there for 10 minutes&nbsp;
before they went on to the next part of the show.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:41.000 --> 00:50:45.720
During that time, I would steal from four people&nbsp;
and pass their items off for the function in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:45.720 --> 00:50:50.720
show. And one person then I would bring up front,&nbsp;
steal all of his stuff while everybody's watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:50.720 --> 00:50:55.640
and give it back. And then later everybody&nbsp;
would find it and it comes back to them later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:55.640 --> 00:51:02.800
I would do that every 10 minutes, six times an&nbsp;
hour, five hours a night, five nights a week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:02.800 --> 00:51:09.720
for five years. So some math, is somewhere around&nbsp;
200,000 people, plus my side gigs, that it became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:09.720 --> 00:51:14.440
a lab for me to experiment. Can I take someone's&nbsp;
glasses while they're wearing them? And what does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:14.440 --> 00:51:19.160
that do? Is it possible? Can I steal their belt?&nbsp;
What are the boundaries? And that was a place for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:19.160 --> 00:51:23.640
me to experiment on the aspect of that.
Tim Ferriss: So could you please&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:23.640 --> 00:51:28.720
tell the story of the Secret Service?
Apollo Robbins: So about probably near the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:28.720 --> 00:51:33.400
about three years after I'd been performing at&nbsp;
Caesars Palace, there was a special night when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:33.400 --> 00:51:39.320
I had come into work and they had a big meeting&nbsp;
before the show. And the manager said, "We have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:39.320 --> 00:51:46.240
a previous president of the United States coming&nbsp;
in to visit and we are going to have some people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:46.240 --> 00:51:49.024
go home that haven't had background checks…"
Tim Ferriss: Slightly different rules.&nbsp;

00:51:49.024 --> 00:51:53.120
Apollo Robbins: Yeah. Because they'd done&nbsp;
background checks on all of us. And he said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:53.120 --> 00:51:57.480
"There'll be photo ops as he goes through the&nbsp;
show. He's got a small party with him. But the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:57.480 --> 00:52:01.400
whole theater is just going to be sectioned off&nbsp;
for that. They have Secret Service will be here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:01.400 --> 00:52:06.680
so don't be alarmed, but they'll be stationed&nbsp;
throughout the casino and throughout the theater."&nbsp;

00:52:06.680 --> 00:52:13.720
And then we were all departing and he said, "But&nbsp;
Apollo, they asked you not to meet the president,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:13.720 --> 00:52:17.000
