01:04:28.960 --> 01:04:32.960
let's call it the performance on video. He says,&nbsp;
"All right, now I'm going to explain how I did&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:32.960 --> 01:04:37.600
it." And he explains it. And then he shows it&nbsp;
again and he says, "Now are you more or less&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:37.600 --> 01:04:41.160
impressed?" I'm paraphrasing here tremendously.
Apollo Robbins: But it was a love story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:41.160 --> 01:04:46.120
right? It turns into a love story with an effect.&nbsp;
That's what I thought was the greater lesson of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:46.120 --> 01:04:51.840
him showing it and exposing it was this beautiful&nbsp;
piece of him coming and spending time with this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:51.840 --> 01:04:55.000
effect and giving part of his life to it.
Tim Ferriss: The second thing I wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:55.000 --> 01:05:03.280
ask about, you have thousands, tens of thousands&nbsp;
of repetitions because you're doing four people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:03.280 --> 01:05:06.627
every, what was it, 20 minutes, 10 minutes?
Apollo Robbins: 10 minutes, yeah.&nbsp;

01:05:06.627 --> 01:05:09.320
Tim Ferriss: Multiple times an hour, over and&nbsp;
over and over again. So you're getting a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:09.320 --> 01:05:16.960
reps. What do you do when it doesn't work? What's&nbsp;
your recovery or how do you handle that? Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:16.960 --> 01:05:22.720
I can't imagine, as you're experimenting, you&nbsp;
must push the envelope, and I imagine, I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:22.720 --> 01:05:27.680
with any skill, it's not going to be a hundred&nbsp;
percent hit rate. So what do you do? Even at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:27.680 --> 01:05:31.040
let's say for instance the Magic Castle, it's&nbsp;
like you see how people recover when things&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:31.040 --> 01:05:32.947
don't work out exactly as planned.
Apollo Robbins: Absolutely.&nbsp;

01:05:32.947 --> 01:05:34.800
Tim Ferriss: For me, that's part of&nbsp;
the art. I mean, it's fascinating.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:34.800 --> 01:05:37.920
So what do you do in those circumstances?
Apollo Robbins: I think there's a subtext&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:37.920 --> 01:05:42.200
to what you're asking that is unique to the&nbsp;
way that the context in which I steal. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:42.200 --> 01:05:47.880
that I have a different social contract&nbsp;
with my audience than a thief does on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:47.880 --> 01:05:55.480
street. So because I can talk to someone,&nbsp;
I get a different access to their body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:55.480 --> 01:06:01.120
I can justify what I'm doing more like — it&nbsp;
functions more like sparring in martial arts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:01.120 --> 01:06:07.480
that if they start to pick up on a thing, there's&nbsp;
a feedback loop that I can see. And the cost of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:07.480 --> 01:06:13.120
them fixating on that catch comes at the cost&nbsp;
of them losing something else. So as soon as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:13.120 --> 01:06:15.080
they start to detect one thing —
Tim Ferriss: Right, if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:15.080 --> 01:06:17.480
cover their head, you go to the body.
Apollo Robbins: Yeah. And once they catch&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:17.480 --> 01:06:21.160
that one thing, they might find themselves three&nbsp;
pounds lighter in other places because they've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:21.160 --> 01:06:24.160
lost all these other things. But there is a very&nbsp;
real — so it changes the definition of fail. Is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:24.160 --> 01:06:30.280
fail getting caught stealing a watch or a belt&nbsp;
or glasses? I was pretty good at rolling with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:30.280 --> 01:06:34.120
that and improvising with those things. And&nbsp;
realizing that if they started to catch that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:34.120 --> 01:06:39.360
I could turn it into another situation.&nbsp;
And I would load strange things on people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:39.360 --> 01:06:44.427
Like I loaded a bag of oregano on a grandma and I&nbsp;
said, "What's this for, Grandma?" And it's like —&nbsp;

01:06:44.427 --> 01:06:50.800
Tim Ferriss: Hold on. This is actually the&nbsp;
perfect place for me to just read an excerpt&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:50.800 --> 01:06:57.160
profile of you, which was by Adam Green&nbsp;
in The New Yorker. And the title of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:57.160 --> 01:07:00.960
I recommend everybody read this, and if you have&nbsp;
anything you'd like to correct in the piece,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:00.960 --> 01:07:05.560
we can cover that too, but “A Pickpocket's Tale:&nbsp;
The Spectacular Thefts of Apollo Robbins.” So this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:05.560 --> 01:07:09.427
is one of my favorite paragraphs —
Apollo Robbins: Fairy tales, yes.&nbsp;

01:07:09.427 --> 01:07:13.320
Tim Ferriss: "When Robbins hits his stride,&nbsp;
it starts to seem as if the only possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:13.320 --> 01:07:18.000
explanation is an ability to start and stop time.&nbsp;
At the Rio, a man’s cell phone disappeared from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:18.000 --> 01:07:22.160
his jacket and was replaced by a piece of&nbsp;
fried chicken; the cigarettes from a pack&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:22.160 --> 01:07:27.200
in one man’s breast pocket materialized loose in&nbsp;
the side pocket of another; a woman’s engagement&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:27.200 --> 01:07:31.960
ring vanished and reappeared attached to a key&nbsp;
ring in her husband’s pants; a man’s driver’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:31.960 --> 01:07:38.520
license disappeared from his wallet and turned up&nbsp;
inside a sealed bag of M&amp;M’s in his wife’s purse."&nbsp;

01:07:38.520 --> 01:07:45.440
Okay. So hard for me to even make any sense&nbsp;
of half of that. But you're loading things&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:45.440 --> 01:07:48.240
on people is where you were.
Apollo Robbins: I am. Yeah. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:48.240 --> 01:07:52.680
take things off, I switch them, I alter them.
Tim Ferriss: So you're creating for yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:52.680 --> 01:07:54.227
options also, right?
Apollo Robbins: Yes.&nbsp;

01:07:54.227 --> 01:07:55.840
Tim Ferriss: It's not like&nbsp;
you have one trick you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:55.840 --> 01:07:59.280
executing and if you flub it, the trick is done?
Apollo Robbins: No. Yeah. That's why I [inaudible&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:59.280 --> 01:08:04.440
01:26:51] sheet music versus jazz. They don't know&nbsp;
where I'm going, so we can always change the path.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:04.440 --> 01:08:09.480
And in fact, when I used to work in situations&nbsp;
where I would just walk around with a group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:09.480 --> 01:08:12.560
which I miss, because I don't really get&nbsp;
to do that, I'm mostly a keynote speaker&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:12.560 --> 01:08:19.320
now. But I used to do a lot of corporate type of&nbsp;
events where I would move through a crowd and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:19.320 --> 01:08:22.360
would walk up to the guys and say, "So guys, you&nbsp;
know why I'm here, right? I'm supposed to steal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:22.360 --> 01:08:28.360
everything you have." And so I'd just say that up&nbsp;
front. It stops most conversations. And I'd say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:28.360 --> 01:08:31.640
"You got anything on you?" And you just see&nbsp;
them check, they would just bump their wallet&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:31.640 --> 01:08:34.560
or that sort of thing, and I'm just clocking.&nbsp;
It's like, "Okay, so I've got the keys here…"&nbsp;

01:08:34.560 --> 01:08:37.520
Tim Ferriss: You know exactly —
Apollo Robbins: Yeah, so I'm making a mental map,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:37.520 --> 01:08:41.120
and now I'm about to move into the circle —
Tim Ferriss: They telegraphed all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:41.120 --> 01:08:42.160
their possessions.
Apollo Robbins: Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:42.160 --> 01:08:47.760
because they don't believe it's true. They just&nbsp;
— we're having a conversation. And now I'm making&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:47.760 --> 01:08:51.000
that effect. It's like, okay, that seems&nbsp;
to be a room key here, that's over here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:51.000 --> 01:08:57.520
this is what here. And as I move through them&nbsp;
— in real pickpocketing, they call it fanning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:57.520 --> 01:09:01.160
Small movements with my elbow or my hand can&nbsp;
give me an idea of what things are in a breast&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:01.160 --> 01:09:04.480
pocket or other things. And I can now —
Tim Ferriss: It's like a shark taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:04.480 --> 01:09:09.520
little nibbling bites, feeling things out.
Apollo Robbins: Yes. And just taking inventory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:09.520 --> 01:09:14.320
you could say. Yeah. And now I'm crafting, okay —
Tim Ferriss: Or a feeling out round in boxing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:14.320 --> 01:09:15.781
same thing, right?
Apollo Robbins: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:09:15.781 --> 01:09:18.424
Tim Ferriss: Where people are gauging&nbsp;
distance, they're seeing how people react.&nbsp;

01:09:18.424 --> 01:09:19.707
Apollo Robbins: What's their&nbsp;
pattern, what's their go to?&nbsp;

01:09:19.707 --> 01:09:21.507
Tim Ferriss: What are their defenses?
Apollo Robbins: Absolutely.&nbsp;

01:09:21.507 --> 01:09:24.440
Tim Ferriss: What are their vulnerabilities, yeah.
Apollo Robbins: Which is interesting because now&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:24.440 --> 01:09:28.800
that I don't have that, I've gone back to martial&nbsp;
arts, because it was one of the few places I could&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:28.800 --> 01:09:33.320
get that feeling. And I was studying a Filipino&nbsp;
style of martial arts, and as I was new in it, one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:33.320 --> 01:09:37.680
of the things that I could do in the first part&nbsp;
was they would go to draw a secondary weapons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:37.680 --> 01:09:43.040
sometimes like a trainer blade, and it wouldn't&nbsp;
be there. You can't do that trick a lot. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:43.040 --> 01:09:46.787
it's a great trick when they go to draw and then&nbsp;
it's not there. They're very surprised. And now —&nbsp;

01:09:46.787 --> 01:09:53.120
Tim Ferriss: I'm sure they're very surprised. All&nbsp;
right. So following this gingerbread trail, I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:53.120 --> 01:09:57.707
wanted to address a couple — all right. Your wife.
Apollo Robbins: Yes.&nbsp;

01:09:57.707 --> 01:10:03.360
Tim Ferriss: So you mentioned I think that&nbsp;
she's the most important influence or the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:03.360 --> 01:10:09.040
biggest impact on you, your career. How so?
Apollo Robbins: Yeah, and on life in general.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:09.040 --> 01:10:14.320
She brought — I think when you're a specialist&nbsp;
in an area, it's easy to get so fixated that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:14.320 --> 01:10:18.760
everything in your world revolves around&nbsp;
that. And mine was all around con artists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:18.760 --> 01:10:25.080
thieves. And I had a soft spot for those guys. I&nbsp;
knew a lot of those guys, were intimate friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:25.080 --> 01:10:28.280
of mine that had done things that — you would&nbsp;
think of like Ocean's Eleven, where they pulled&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:28.280 --> 01:10:32.920
heists on casinos and switched out devices&nbsp;
and things. And I knew those guys well. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:32.920 --> 01:10:38.480
that was my world. It was my cadre of team.
And around that time I had this weird inquiry&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:38.480 --> 01:10:43.840
to do a TV show about picking up women. Just&nbsp;
because as a side thing people said, "Hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:43.840 --> 01:10:49.560
you seem to be really good at this thing." And I&nbsp;
wasn't ever a person who did notches on the belt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:49.560 --> 01:10:54.520
but it's another thing, the perspective&nbsp;
taking, it's related to that and how you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:54.520 --> 01:10:59.200
establish rapport and things. And there's a friend&nbsp;
of mine who was taught hostage negotiation. He had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:59.200 --> 01:11:03.640
a special forces background. He says, "Hey,&nbsp;
why don't we do this book together and we'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:03.640 --> 01:11:07.120
go shoot this video and we'll ask women how&nbsp;
they would like to be approached. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:07.120 --> 01:11:11.040
we'll put hidden cameras on them and see when&nbsp;
people approach them, how ridiculous people do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:11.040 --> 01:11:16.480
approach them. And then we'll talk about that."
And I said, "Sounds like a great idea." And he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:16.480 --> 01:11:20.600
calls me, he's got a film crew coming in and&nbsp;
some models and other things, and he says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:20.600 --> 01:11:26.120
"Hey, we're having this thing, late night, at a&nbsp;
party in Las Vegas, at a all night spot." He says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:26.120 --> 01:11:29.240
"Come." I said, "It's too late for me." He&nbsp;
says, "No, just come." And when I show up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:29.240 --> 01:11:36.200
he's talking to a group of women. And I thought&nbsp;
they were probably escorts at that point.&nbsp;

01:11:36.200 --> 01:11:37.600
Tim Ferriss: Professionals.
Apollo Robbins: Yeah. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:37.600 --> 01:11:41.800
they were close to the VIP section. And it was a&nbsp;
small group, younger women, some older guys at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:41.800 --> 01:11:46.680
table. And I'd seen that a lot in Vegas. But when&nbsp;
I get closer and I'm talking to them, and one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:46.680 --> 01:11:51.920
the ladies in the group, as they were introducing&nbsp;
themselves, she had a background in psychobiology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:51.920 --> 01:11:56.440
she had just graduated with a degree in that.&nbsp;
She was working as a crisis counselor on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:56.440 --> 01:12:02.160
hotline talking people off the cliff. She would&nbsp;
do some pretty intense stuff and studied abnormal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:02.160 --> 01:12:07.840
psych and very kind of intense and playful.&nbsp;
And he said I was a thief when he introduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:07.840 --> 01:12:10.720
me to this group of girls. And I stole —
Tim Ferriss: It is a hell of an intro,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:10.720 --> 01:12:13.400
you're going to get a conversation.
Apollo Robbins: It does. One of them said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:13.400 --> 01:12:16.080
"Can I..." So I stole the girl's&nbsp;
engagement ring with the thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:16.080 --> 01:12:23.560
and I did a thing with that and gave it back. And&nbsp;
this lady, that later turned out to be my wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:23.560 --> 01:12:26.080
she asked me, "Can you steal something me&nbsp;
for me?" I said, "What would you like?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:26.080 --> 01:12:30.840
She says, "A chocolate-covered strawberry from&nbsp;
that table over there." And she points at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:30.840 --> 01:12:36.120
table nearby, a VIP section. I said, "Got it."
So I grabbed a menu, walked over, pretended to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:36.120 --> 01:12:39.680
with the staff, asked them if they're being taken&nbsp;
care of, stole the chocolate strawberry under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:39.680 --> 01:12:43.200
menu. And then I walked to her and then I threw it&nbsp;
over my shoulder to add a little flair, I caught&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:43.200 --> 01:12:46.920
it. And went to hand it to her. And when she went&nbsp;
to take a bite of it, I put it in my mouth and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:46.920 --> 01:12:50.720
just winked at her. So she'd have to kiss me to&nbsp;
get the strawberry. And it's that playfulness I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:50.720 --> 01:12:52.600
think that's the important thing.
Tim Ferriss:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:52.600 --> 01:12:56.240
It's bold. Bold. I like it. But it worked.
Apollo Robbins: It is. Yeah. But it almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:56.240 --> 01:13:04.280
didn't work because her advocacy and her academia,&nbsp;
and it was the opposite, it was too slick. And she&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:04.280 --> 01:13:07.840
felt that pressure from the group. She gave me&nbsp;
the kiss, but we ended up leaning against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:07.840 --> 01:13:11.200
wall talking about psychology. And that dove&nbsp;
into a deep thing. She said, "Have you ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:11.200 --> 01:13:15.480
thought about this..." Long phone conversations&nbsp;
later on. But she said, "Have you ever thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:15.480 --> 01:13:19.360
about all this stuff you're learning and how&nbsp;
it applies to bigger problems in the world?"&nbsp;

01:13:19.360 --> 01:13:24.080
And she was the one that encouraged me to do the&nbsp;
event with the neuroscientist. She was the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:24.080 --> 01:13:28.560
that encouraged me to take the piece with The&nbsp;
New Yorker, which I didn't even know what The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:28.560 --> 01:13:33.360
New Yorker was at that point. I turned it down&nbsp;
twice. Adam Green would tell you. I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:33.360 --> 01:13:36.840
"Here, somebody better over here." It&nbsp;
wasn't my thing. I didn't even barely&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:36.840 --> 01:13:40.720
knew who the politicians were. It wasn't&nbsp;
my world. I didn't think about that.&nbsp;

01:13:40.720 --> 01:13:46.240
She said, "Let me have you watch a debate."&nbsp;
And when I saw my first presidential debate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:46.240 --> 01:13:49.840
I went back to Teller, I said, "Teller, you're a&nbsp;
Latin professor, what was that?" I said, "Why do I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:49.840 --> 01:13:56.040
know all those things? They're so similar to this&nbsp;
thing that the Yellow Kid used to do or Titanic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:56.040 --> 01:13:59.800
Thompson used to do this thing with this."
Tim Ferriss: Wait a second. Meaning you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:59.800 --> 01:14:04.664
observed a lot in the debate that&nbsp;
paralleled what you had learned.&nbsp;

01:14:04.664 --> 01:14:06.343
Apollo Robbins: Very similar&nbsp;
concepts. And he said —&nbsp;

01:14:06.343 --> 01:14:10.400
Tim Ferriss: Can you give an example?
Apollo Robbins: There is a term called Gish&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:10.400 --> 01:14:14.264
galloping. I forgot the guy's name for Gish.
Tim Ferriss: Gish galloping.&nbsp;

01:14:14.264 --> 01:14:19.240
Apollo Robbins: Gish was a debater who would often&nbsp;
do a package of assaults on a person, or of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:19.240 --> 01:14:23.360
human character, where it should be. He'd say,&nbsp;
during his three-minute window of a debate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:23.360 --> 01:14:27.400
he would say, "My adversary, my opponent, would&nbsp;
have you believe this, this, this, this, this."&nbsp;

01:14:27.400 --> 01:14:34.000
And then the opponent, his countermeasure for&nbsp;
that, becomes, "Well..." They're overwhelmed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:34.000 --> 01:14:36.600
And they don't know how to respond, because&nbsp;
they've got three minutes, and he posited&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:36.600 --> 01:14:41.040
a lot of questions, and they don't have three&nbsp;
minutes, functionally, to answer those questions.&nbsp;

01:14:41.040 --> 01:14:44.720
And that, very similar to me, was a&nbsp;
noise-to-signal ratio piece that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:44.720 --> 01:14:49.560
used to overwhelm people inside of&nbsp;
a cash exchange with shortchanging.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:49.560 --> 01:14:55.200
And I recognize that as one approach.
Tim Ferriss: Shortchanging. Giving somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:55.200 --> 01:14:59.120
change, but less change than they should get back?
Apollo Robbins: And overwhelming a cash register&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:59.120 --> 01:15:02.800
attendee at that time. And using&nbsp;
ambiguous statements with different&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:02.800 --> 01:15:07.480
ways that they could take that as for&nbsp;
— and I can clarify more on that later.&nbsp;

01:15:07.480 --> 01:15:13.760
But a counter to that Gish gallop,&nbsp;
for example, back to equivoque for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:13.760 --> 01:15:18.680
equivoke. If somebody were doing a series&nbsp;
of personal assassinations of — they also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:18.680 --> 01:15:24.200
what they're doing, is diluting their argument.
Because now, if I say 10 bad things about you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:24.200 --> 01:15:27.400
or you said that about me, then I'd say,&nbsp;
"As you can see, he's very impassioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:27.400 --> 01:15:30.800
about what he says." But notice there's a&nbsp;
central theme between all of those. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:30.800 --> 01:15:36.360
central theme is X. And that's the equivoque.
I can take all the things that you said, repackage&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:36.360 --> 01:15:40.120
them as another thing, and because you diluted&nbsp;
all your points, because you put them all into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:40.120 --> 01:15:43.760
the one bracket,. so the countermeasure to that&nbsp;
is to dilute that down, give you one point back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:43.760 --> 01:15:45.760
and I hijack the narrative.
Tim Ferriss: All right. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:45.760 --> 01:15:51.067
you're talking to Teller about this.
Apollo Robbins: Yes. And he was a Latin professor.&nbsp;

01:15:51.067 --> 01:15:53.360
Tim Ferriss: Okay. That wasn't&nbsp;
— I thought that was a euphemism&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:53.360 --> 01:15:57.040
or a joke. He was an actual Latin professor?
Apollo Robbins: He was. Before, in his career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:57.040 --> 01:16:03.080
I said that, "You know more about Socrates and&nbsp;
Plato, so can you tell me?" And he says, "Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:03.080 --> 01:16:08.040
you need to go back, read Plato's Republic. You&nbsp;
need to study about Socrates." And he started&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:08.040 --> 01:16:11.080
prompting me to go back to learn this root.
Tim Ferriss: That's amazing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:11.080 --> 01:16:13.467
Yeah. Back to the classics.
Apollo Robbins: Back to the classics.&nbsp;

01:16:13.467 --> 01:16:23.120
Tim Ferriss: So, from the strawberry in the&nbsp;
mouth to broader applications in psychology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:23.120 --> 01:16:27.960
or at least harnessing psychology, recognizing&nbsp;
maybe the common threads across multiple domains&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:27.960 --> 01:16:34.000
that you'd already been exposed to, and had&nbsp;
polished in your craft — to presidential&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:34.000 --> 01:16:38.880
debate, to then reading Plato and Aristotle.
Apollo Robbins: Yes. And the driver of this was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:38.880 --> 01:16:45.800
back to that, was my wife. We had this unusual&nbsp;
thing in that she moved to Las Vegas pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

