01:28:57.640 --> 01:29:03.160
metaphor for a con. Right? That one little ring.&nbsp;
Because that whole story that you jumped into,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:03.160 --> 01:29:07.520
that little rabbit hole with asking about the&nbsp;
ring, is a great baseline that now people assume&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:07.520 --> 01:29:11.280
there's one, and there's that baseline piece.
But to me, that is that greater question that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:11.280 --> 01:29:16.400
when we're succeeding, we're often not curious&nbsp;
why we're having success. And it's a very unusual&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:16.400 --> 01:29:19.587
thing for us to say, "Well, let me check&nbsp;
out the other hand," when you win. Right?&nbsp;

01:29:19.587 --> 01:29:21.360
Tim Ferriss: When you win, yeah.
Apollo Robbins: Yeah. But that's confirmation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:21.360 --> 01:29:22.820
bias. And it's really helpful —
Tim Ferriss: 100 percent.&nbsp;

01:29:22.820 --> 01:29:27.560
Apollo Robbins: — to know what that is. And it's&nbsp;
very easy. You didn't jump into the hole like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:27.560 --> 01:29:31.640
most people do. Most people will say, "Well, I've&nbsp;
played poker with my grandma since I was four. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:31.640 --> 01:29:35.560
really good at reading people." And they walk&nbsp;
away with this confidence. And that's the con&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:35.560 --> 01:29:38.424
aspect of that. But in this one —
Tim Ferriss: Right. Confidence.&nbsp;

01:29:38.424 --> 01:29:39.944
Apollo Robbins: It is.
Tim Ferriss: Confidence man, confidence.&nbsp;

01:29:39.944 --> 01:29:42.680
Apollo Robbins: You see me playing low status&nbsp;
with that. I need you to miss, in order for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:42.680 --> 01:29:48.907
me to be able to — but that kind of thing,&nbsp;
everything is about helping people get to that.&nbsp;

01:29:48.907 --> 01:29:56.360
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. All right. That was a great&nbsp;
demo. And that actually ties into the question of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:56.360 --> 01:30:00.440
can you elaborate on what makes your style.&nbsp;
How is it different from what people think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:00.440 --> 01:30:06.920
of as magic, maybe what they've seen growing up?
Apollo Robbins: I'll give you an experience one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:06.920 --> 01:30:13.200
time. Where I had — I'll let you just see kind&nbsp;
of backstage. One time I was performing for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:13.200 --> 01:30:18.720
corporate event in Las Vegas. And it's similar&nbsp;
to the one where I had the broken watch piece,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:18.720 --> 01:30:23.920
but this is a bit later in my career.
And it was a new agent, and I was talking to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:23.920 --> 01:30:27.600
her off to the side. And I met a couple of the&nbsp;
people that were there, one of the ladies who&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:27.600 --> 01:30:33.240
ran the thing named Lisa, several other people.
But then this other guy came up. Well-dressed guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:33.240 --> 01:30:37.520
and he said, "So, I heard you steal things. Is&nbsp;
that right?" And I said, "Yeah, something like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:37.520 --> 01:30:41.800
that." "So steal something from me." I said,&nbsp;
"I'm sorry. What was your name?" He says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:41.800 --> 01:30:46.680
"Jamal." I said, "Nice to meet you, Jamal."
And before this, let's go back. That I walked&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:46.680 --> 01:30:52.080
into this event thinking, "I don't want to do&nbsp;
anything I've done before, exactly." There's some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:52.080 --> 01:30:56.200
little components, and you'll hear some things&nbsp;
coming back where I just talked about with Penn.&nbsp;

01:30:56.200 --> 01:31:03.240
But I knew that I could figure out how&nbsp;
to put something inside of a bag of M&amp;Ms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:03.240 --> 01:31:07.680
and then reseal it in real time without&nbsp;
anybody noticing. And I thought, "What could&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:07.680 --> 01:31:13.800
I do with that? Let's just find out."
And that's where it started to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:13.800 --> 01:31:18.080
a jazz. So this interaction with this guy,&nbsp;
Jamal, when he just came up, he says, "So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:18.080 --> 01:31:22.320
you can steal something from me?" And I said, "I&nbsp;
don't rightly know. What was your name?" He goes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:22.320 --> 01:31:25.800
"Jamal." I said, "How do you spell that,&nbsp;
Jamal?" And he said, "J, A…" I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:25.800 --> 01:31:28.320
"Could you write that down for me?"
And he pulls out his pen and he goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:28.320 --> 01:31:36.080
to write it down, and his pen wasn't working,&nbsp;
as we've heard before. And up to this point, my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:36.080 --> 01:31:41.427
stuttering, the break of my speech, the break of&nbsp;
my sight lines with him, has been very low status.&nbsp;

01:31:41.427 --> 01:31:43.384
Tim Ferriss: Break of your&nbsp;
sight lines meaning eye contact?&nbsp;

01:31:43.384 --> 01:31:46.080
Apollo Robbins: Yeah. I didn't keep straight&nbsp;
eye contact with him very much. I asked him&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:46.080 --> 01:31:50.120
about his suit, "That's really fancy." It&nbsp;
was all low status. And then everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:50.120 --> 01:31:54.240
just changed. Then it's this thing. I&nbsp;
said, "Would this help you out, Jamal?"&nbsp;

01:31:54.240 --> 01:31:59.320
And it's straight eye contact, and it's the refill&nbsp;
to his ink pen. And he had way underestimated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:59.320 --> 01:32:04.600
and stepped way too close, he realized&nbsp;
suddenly. And when that moment happened to him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:04.600 --> 01:32:07.680
you saw him holding his pen with one&nbsp;
hand and his refill with the other. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:07.680 --> 01:32:10.520
he was just looking, and he's stunned.
He's in this cycle of trying to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:10.520 --> 01:32:14.720
that — because the only solution it could be&nbsp;
is the one he didn't want to accept. So he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:14.720 --> 01:32:18.800
canceled out the truth. Where is he going to go&nbsp;
from there? That I had stolen it, taken it apart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:18.800 --> 01:32:21.680
put it back together, and put it back on him.
Couldn't be that. So what else could it be?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:22.240 --> 01:32:27.600
Pausing time, time travel. So in that, now&nbsp;
at that moment, while he's in that bewildered&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:27.600 --> 01:32:31.240
state, I just said, "Well, at least you&nbsp;
didn't lose your money or your wallet."&nbsp;

01:32:31.240 --> 01:32:34.360
And when I did that, I just glanced down. Because&nbsp;
I noticed he has his watch on his left wrist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:34.360 --> 01:32:36.440
so he's probably right-handed. So he's&nbsp;
probably going to keep his wallet in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:36.440 --> 01:32:41.360
back right. So I just glanced back, toward his&nbsp;
back right pocket. And he jumped on that big.&nbsp;

01:32:41.360 --> 01:32:44.000
He reached in, pulled out his wallet real&nbsp;
quick, checked to see if he had everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:44.000 --> 01:32:47.720
still in his wallet. And I laughed, and&nbsp;
I saw his attention shift back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:47.720 --> 01:32:52.600
pen. And I pushed down his hand. I said, "You&nbsp;
still have everything." And he put it away.&nbsp;

01:32:52.600 --> 01:32:56.160
And he says, "You've got to do this to Lisa." I&nbsp;
said, "Yeah, great to meet her." I just looked&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:56.160 --> 01:33:01.880
over his shoulder to where I thought Lisa might&nbsp;
be. And he took off to go get Lisa. Comes back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:01.880 --> 01:33:05.427
and he shoves this lady towards&nbsp;
me, and this agent. And I'm —&nbsp;

01:33:05.427 --> 01:33:08.440
Tim Ferriss: "Dance, monkey. Dance."
Apollo Robbins: Yeah. Well, I'm there to perform.&nbsp;

01:33:08.440 --> 01:33:11.080
Tim Ferriss: No, I know. I know.
Apollo Robbins: And I'm talking to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:11.080 --> 01:33:15.040
agent. I have a drink. And this is an important&nbsp;
thing, the drink, just because it's a part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:15.040 --> 01:33:19.680
the style. It's not alcohol, it's just water.
But it's that I don't need anything from him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:19.680 --> 01:33:22.760
I'm not being validated. And a lot of&nbsp;
performers will try to spoon feed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:22.760 --> 01:33:26.160
audience. But it's letting them bring a thing to&nbsp;
them. Letting them have a reaction without you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:26.160 --> 01:33:30.800
needing to feed back off of it. And letting&nbsp;
people have this experience for themselves.&nbsp;

01:33:30.800 --> 01:33:34.800
So when he pushed the lady up, I didn't quite look&nbsp;
at her at first. I said, "Sorry. It's nice to meet&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:34.800 --> 01:33:38.200
you." I looked over and I said, "What was your&nbsp;
name?" She goes, "Lisa." And she hadn't quite come&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:38.200 --> 01:33:43.720
close to me at all. She said, "What's happening?"
I said, "Sorry, Lisa. He wants me to steal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:43.720 --> 01:33:48.160
something from you." She says, "What?" I said,&nbsp;
"Do you like chocolate?" She says, "Chocolate?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:48.160 --> 01:33:52.680
I said, "Yes. There's some chocolate in your&nbsp;
purse." She said, "What?" She opens her purse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:52.680 --> 01:33:58.320
and she finds a bag of M&amp;Ms in her purse.
And he's reacting very strongly to this. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:58.320 --> 01:34:02.320
he's watching this third party, he thinks.&nbsp;
And then she said, "How did those get in my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:02.320 --> 01:34:06.160
purse?" And I said, "The same way Jamal's&nbsp;
driver's license got out of his wallet."&nbsp;

01:34:06.160 --> 01:34:09.360
Now he's jumped back into the equation.&nbsp;
He rips his wallet out again, realizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:09.360 --> 01:34:14.400
his license is missing. And he goes, "Where in&nbsp;
the fuck is my driver's license?" And I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:14.400 --> 01:34:17.760
"It's in the chocolate," as I take a&nbsp;
sip of my water. And I just walk away.&nbsp;

01:34:17.760 --> 01:34:21.240
So I let them piece it together&nbsp;
themselves. She tears open the M&amp;Ms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:21.240 --> 01:34:27.720
and she finds the license in her thing.
Tim Ferriss: Okay. If we zoom out, then, amazing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:27.720 --> 01:34:35.640
story. I would have to think 100 percent, maybe&nbsp;
99.999 percent of people listening, have never&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:35.640 --> 01:34:42.320
experienced something like this. What are the&nbsp;
ingredients? You mentioned some of them. Are there&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:42.320 --> 01:34:49.024
other ingredients that you can call out explicitly&nbsp;
that are some of the hallmarks that differentiate?&nbsp;

01:34:49.024 --> 01:34:52.400
Apollo Robbins: Yeah. And part of&nbsp;
that that's quite different in magic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:52.400 --> 01:34:57.360
is it tends to be that they'll have a set of&nbsp;
effects that are linked together. In that one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:57.360 --> 01:35:01.760
I walked in trying — I had a set of skills that&nbsp;
I could do, but I hadn't pieced them together.&nbsp;

01:35:01.760 --> 01:35:06.800
And I take advantage of the movement, like&nbsp;
you would if you were sparring in that way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:06.800 --> 01:35:11.360
When he leaned in forward, I stepped back with my&nbsp;
left foot. And I said, "It's nice to meet you."&nbsp;

01:35:11.360 --> 01:35:14.480
But at the same time as my right hand&nbsp;
comes up, with the back of my hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:14.480 --> 01:35:19.680
I kind of put my hand towards his chest as he came&nbsp;
in a little bit too close, my left hand came up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:19.680 --> 01:35:24.000
underneath the sight line and slipped inside of&nbsp;
his jacket, and took out his pen really quick.&nbsp;

01:35:24.000 --> 01:35:28.600
And I went down the line of my arm and into&nbsp;
my pocket, so he couldn't see anything being&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:28.600 --> 01:35:32.960
extracted. And then I stepped back. And then I&nbsp;
removed the pen, took it apart, and I find another&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:32.960 --> 01:35:35.680
reason to come back in to put it back there.
Tim Ferriss: It's so outrageous that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:35.680 --> 01:35:39.080
can do any of this. It's amazing,
Apollo Robbins: But it's a strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:39.080 --> 01:35:43.000
thing. And then, each one of those moments,&nbsp;
on the backside of that, is I didn't know what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:43.000 --> 01:35:48.880
was going to happen. But what I saw is when his&nbsp;
attention locked to that pen, and he was spinning.&nbsp;

01:35:48.880 --> 01:35:55.280
And you can think of attention as being external&nbsp;
or internal, inside your head or external,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:55.280 --> 01:36:00.280
aware. And you can think of it as being broad or&nbsp;
narrow. And on those axes, it's very useful of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:00.280 --> 01:36:04.000
"Do I want him to be broad/external or&nbsp;
do I want him to be internal/narrow?"&nbsp;

01:36:04.000 --> 01:36:09.160
And how do I flip somebody from one of those to&nbsp;
the other? And I'm very attuned to doing that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:09.160 --> 01:36:14.080
of moving people between those four stages. So&nbsp;
that was a big part there, was to do that to him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:14.080 --> 01:36:21.160
And when he pulled that out, and he thought he had&nbsp;
the money, I just copped his driver's license out&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:21.160 --> 01:36:25.640
of his wallet and let him put his wallet back.
But he was already still thinking about the pen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:25.640 --> 01:36:28.800
so I could see he was thinking about&nbsp;
the pen. So when I pushed down his hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:28.800 --> 01:36:32.120
I just took the wallet out. The problem is&nbsp;
his train of thought would go straight back&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:32.120 --> 01:36:35.680
to it if I reveal, "Oh, I've got your&nbsp;
license," he would go straight to it.&nbsp;

01:36:35.680 --> 01:36:40.680
So I thought, "I guess I'm going to put this&nbsp;
license inside the M&amp;Ms. Let me see how I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:40.680 --> 01:36:44.520
do that." And as I'm thinking about that, he&nbsp;
said, "Well, you should meet Lisa." I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:44.520 --> 01:36:46.024
"What a great idea."
Tim Ferriss: Perfect.&nbsp;

01:36:46.024 --> 01:36:49.160
Apollo Robbins: Yes. And I think she's the one&nbsp;
I met. Maybe there's more Lisas, but let's see&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:49.160 --> 01:36:53.000
where he goes. And I said, "Yeah, that'd be nice&nbsp;
to meet her." So he goes off, and he takes off to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:53.000 --> 01:36:59.360
get Lisa. I see another Lisa that I have met.
