lubricant. It happens all the time around us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:11.920 --> 01:40:16.360
from the moment we put on makeup to something&nbsp;
else. So, whether it be a defeat for cancer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:16.360 --> 01:40:21.240
by using decoy cells, there's all these&nbsp;
things that have a reason. But it's about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:21.240 --> 01:40:25.520
your intent, and that's the big thing.
Tim Ferriss: Could you say more about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:25.520 --> 01:40:31.480
that? Because that would be very heretical&nbsp;
to, I'm sure, some people listening. Like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:31.480 --> 01:40:33.267
"Wait a second. No. Deception bad."
Apollo Robbins: Yes.&nbsp;

01:40:33.267 --> 01:40:34.227
Tim Ferriss: Right?
Apollo Robbins: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:40:34.227 --> 01:40:39.680
Tim Ferriss: No exceptions. Black and white.&nbsp;
That's the dark side of the Force. Could you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:39.680 --> 01:40:47.480
say a bit more about how you teach your&nbsp;
child, your children, about deception? Or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:47.480 --> 01:40:52.360
if you want to tackle this a different way,&nbsp;
is there a pro-social or a positive use of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:52.360 --> 01:40:56.240
deception, and a way to frame that for people?
Apollo Robbins: Absolutely. And that's definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:56.240 --> 01:41:00.880
the flag I fly under now. Is I think that&nbsp;
there's value in learning about deception.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:01.520 --> 01:41:07.800
One of the reasons why we have critical thinking&nbsp;
is to counter deception. And it's helpful now when&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:07.800 --> 01:41:11.720
people say, "We need more critical thinking," to&nbsp;
understand what was the original reason for it.&nbsp;

