the hardest pocket to steal from because they&nbsp;
would call it kissing the dog. That was the old&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:24.120 --> 02:07:30.960
term. Kissing the dog meant that I would have to&nbsp;
see the mark's face. With most other extractions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:30.960 --> 02:07:35.640
you wouldn't get to see me. My team would&nbsp;
lock you up. I would work in a blind spot&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:35.640 --> 02:07:39.760
angle and I would do an extraction that way. If&nbsp;
I had to steal from inside your jacket pocket,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:39.760 --> 02:07:45.920
I've come up with a few ways to extract that&nbsp;
without you having to see my face per se. I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:45.920 --> 02:07:48.520
show you maybe some of that a little bit.
Tim Ferriss: Headbutting the dog.&nbsp;

02:07:48.520 --> 02:07:52.040
Apollo Robbins: Yes, headbutting the dog. Make&nbsp;
sure they don't remember. So inside jacket&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:52.040 --> 02:07:58.160
pocket is a spot. I think it's more of when&nbsp;
you take things out to make a payment, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:58.160 --> 02:08:02.200
useful to leave something there. There's a great&nbsp;
— a thing that often beats security is what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:02.200 --> 02:08:07.240
call satisfaction of search where they're looking&nbsp;
for a thing, an anomaly. They find something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:07.240 --> 02:08:11.320
satisfies that and they stop looking. You&nbsp;
can reverse that and use that for thieves.&nbsp;

02:08:11.320 --> 02:08:15.440
Give them something to find. If you're&nbsp;
going to go make a purchase out somewhere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:15.440 --> 02:08:18.800
quarantine a portion of your money. Any valuables&nbsp;
that you have, sentimental or other things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:18.800 --> 02:08:23.520
don't wear them publicly. If you're moving&nbsp;
into any kind of isolated spot, it's not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:23.520 --> 02:08:29.200
pickpockets, it's for being mugged too. You&nbsp;
can hide them in plain sight. So for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:29.200 --> 02:08:32.787
what is your EDC? What are the things you carry&nbsp;
with you? Now more and more people are carrying —&nbsp;

02:08:32.787 --> 02:08:34.280
Tim Ferriss: Everyday carry?
Apollo Robbins: Yeah, EDC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:34.280 --> 02:08:40.400
everyday carry and definitely a pickpocket's&nbsp;
library. Your water bottle, a coffee cup,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:40.400 --> 02:08:46.280
those things. If I take things that are of value,&nbsp;
sentimental value, an improvised safe could be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:46.280 --> 02:08:51.240
coffee cup. I put these items inside of a coffee&nbsp;
cup, put a lid on it. Now even if you rob me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:51.880 --> 02:08:56.560
taser me, and strip me down, you probably&nbsp;
won't search in that coffee cup. That's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:56.560 --> 02:09:03.080
mindset that I'm talking about. So the idea of&nbsp;
treating the unimportant as important and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:03.080 --> 02:09:09.440
important as unimportant, you want to simplify it.
I think you can take that to your hotel room. Are&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:09.440 --> 02:09:13.480
you going to buy some kind of stash concealment&nbsp;
that looks like after shave or you're going to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:13.480 --> 02:09:18.440
those things? Instead of leave something there&nbsp;
in the safe because you need to satiate their&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:18.440 --> 02:09:23.120
curiosity. You need to give the dog a bone to&nbsp;
find. You need to, as Herb Simon in behavioral&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:23.120 --> 02:09:27.907
economics, he would call it satisficing. You&nbsp;
need something that satisfies their curiosity.&nbsp;

02:09:27.907 --> 02:09:32.998
Tim Ferriss: I'm glad I asked. Thank you.&nbsp;
What is The Illusion of Knowledge Project?&nbsp;

02:09:32.998 --> 02:09:37.400
Apollo Robbins: That is a current effort. So we&nbsp;
have a new company that we're trying to create.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:37.400 --> 02:09:43.760
I've kind of tiptoed into that as we've been&nbsp;
speaking about because it's a soft business model.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:43.760 --> 02:09:49.280
It's still a shape of what the market is for that.&nbsp;
We think it's important to point the finger at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:49.280 --> 02:09:53.320
problem that we see on the horizon. So I'm trying&nbsp;
to find — the benefit is I have an amazing network&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:53.320 --> 02:09:57.080
of people that I've learned from. It used to&nbsp;
be thieves, but now it's security specialists&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:57.080 --> 02:10:02.040
and other ones, and just specialists around the&nbsp;
world that are incredible experts in these areas.&nbsp;

02:10:02.040 --> 02:10:06.520
I was saying, my background's in entertainment.&nbsp;
We did some TV works, different things. Can we&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:06.520 --> 02:10:12.720
repackage shows, whether it's TV or live&nbsp;
experience or content that can help people&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:12.720 --> 02:10:15.240
engage with things that they might not think&nbsp;
should be important, that they might not think are&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:15.240 --> 02:10:20.240
important, their data, their other things? Can you&nbsp;
reverse a room escape and make it a heist? While&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:20.240 --> 02:10:24.787
people are trying to break into a thing they learn&nbsp;
about their personal data, these kind of examples.&nbsp;

02:10:24.787 --> 02:10:26.680
Tim Ferriss: That's a fun idea.
Apollo Robbins: Yeah, it probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:26.680 --> 02:10:30.320
is now stolen that I've said it out loud.
Tim Ferriss: We could add in a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:30.320 --> 02:10:31.240
bleeps if you like.
Apollo Robbins: Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:31.240 --> 02:10:36.400
no, leave it in there. It's just karma for me&nbsp;
doing all the things I've done. It's the kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:36.400 --> 02:10:42.000
of things that give you those ideas. So we did a&nbsp;
project along that, it was a video project called&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:42.000 --> 02:10:46.040
The Illusion of Knowledge. It's this Daniel&nbsp;
Boorstin quote that "The enemy of knowledge is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:46.040 --> 02:10:52.160
not ignorance, it's the illusion of knowledge."
That marries so well with my background growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:52.160 --> 02:10:58.840
up around cons. Since my job has always been&nbsp;
instilling confidence, not just the extraction of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:58.840 --> 02:11:04.800
personal items from a person, it's getting people&nbsp;
to make other decisions. As I learn about that, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:04.800 --> 02:11:10.040
think it's very important for people to understand&nbsp;
that we are all puppets. We all have strings.&nbsp;

02:11:10.040 --> 02:11:14.160
It's a benefit for people to know that about&nbsp;
themselves instead of saying, "But I'm not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:14.160 --> 02:11:18.640
puppet," and realize that they do have a string&nbsp;
and what does it mean for somebody to pull that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:18.640 --> 02:11:24.320
string. That awareness of what it looks like in&nbsp;
different ways that those strings can be pulled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:24.320 --> 02:11:29.120
I think can help bring people so that the flags go&nbsp;
up. So we're creating this project, The Illusion&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:29.120 --> 02:11:33.120
Knowledge Project, to highlight some of that.
Tim Ferriss: Where can people find that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:33.120 --> 02:11:34.840
or can they not yet find it?
Apollo Robbins: They can't find&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:34.840 --> 02:11:44.320
it yet. There's a trailer. We have this website&nbsp;
called Equivoke. Equivoke is E-Q-U-I-V-O-K-E. Or&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:44.320 --> 02:11:51.480
they can go to my website. It's easy. My website&nbsp;
is istealstuff.com and it's easier for people to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:51.480 --> 02:11:55.640
remember while they're driving. If you go to&nbsp;
that, there'll be a link to Equivoke. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:55.640 --> 02:12:00.360
a link to those things, explore and poke around.
I don't have much content out there that I put&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:00.360 --> 02:12:05.360
as products because right now I'm at a phase&nbsp;
where I have two kids. I have one that's 30,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:05.360 --> 02:12:09.400
one that's six. I want to try to do the thing&nbsp;
that I can to change the world in the way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:09.400 --> 02:12:14.800
I can. So a lot of these are legacy type things&nbsp;
that build it first, build business model later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:14.800 --> 02:12:19.720
which is probably not the best approach.&nbsp;
It's something that I'm doing right now.&nbsp;

02:12:19.720 --> 02:12:25.640
Tim Ferriss: It may not always be the most&nbsp;
failsafe approach, but it is, I think the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:25.640 --> 02:12:32.120
approach that I see aligned with intention&nbsp;
and integrity very often in people who are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:32.120 --> 02:12:39.600
and I mean this in the most complimentary sense&nbsp;
possible, high-level craftsmen or craftswomen. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:39.600 --> 02:12:46.240
it's not impossible to do. It can be difficult to&nbsp;
do. But I don't think it's an incorrect approach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:46.240 --> 02:12:47.627
Does that make sense?
Apollo Robbins: Yes.&nbsp;

02:12:47.627 --> 02:12:55.080
Tim Ferriss: Because you can, conversely&nbsp;
— look, I invest in a lot of, or I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:55.080 --> 02:12:59.640
historically invested in a lot of startups and&nbsp;
early stage companies and so on. And it can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:59.640 --> 02:13:04.304
incredibly profitable to start with a model&nbsp;
and then figure out what fits around that.&nbsp;

02:13:04.304 --> 02:13:07.960
Apollo Robbins: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: However, when you have a craft and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:07.960 --> 02:13:14.920
when you have the refined perception and ability&nbsp;
to operate in the world that you have, that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:14.920 --> 02:13:20.280
can expand into these different domains, I think&nbsp;
that approach makes a lot of sense. I really do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:20.280 --> 02:13:26.504
Because you can corrupt the sincerity of the&nbsp;
knowledge you've accumulated by forcing it —&nbsp;

02:13:26.504 --> 02:13:29.024
Apollo Robbins: Into a model.
Tim Ferriss: — into a model. Yeah. Anyway.&nbsp;

02:13:29.024 --> 02:13:33.520
Apollo Robbins: Well said. I think, I don't know&nbsp;
if the right metaphor of spoke and hub, but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:33.520 --> 02:13:40.000
is a little bit like that. I have different ways&nbsp;
I've been able to make a very good living through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:40.000 --> 02:13:45.280
whether it's speaking or doing workshops or doing&nbsp;
training or creating things to teach security. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:45.280 --> 02:13:52.960
as I'm doing those, since I got my boxes checked,&nbsp;
I felt, okay, let's be exploratory. What else can&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:52.960 --> 02:13:58.960
I use this thing that I have, this network that&nbsp;
I have? And instead, can it be something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:58.960 --> 02:14:04.320
is using the fun part of where I came from?
Tim Ferriss: So I have to ask, because this&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:04.320 --> 02:14:07.187
is just my brain won't let it go.
Apollo Robbins: Yeah.&nbsp;

02:14:07.187 --> 02:14:13.400
Tim Ferriss: So the 40 Elephants, I just love&nbsp;
this name. Are there other historical figures&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:13.400 --> 02:14:22.000
or bands of merry pranksters/criminals who&nbsp;
have particularly memorable names or stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:22.000 --> 02:14:27.840
for you? If you had to put together your&nbsp;
League of Legends, is there a shortlist?&nbsp;

02:14:27.840 --> 02:14:33.000
Apollo Robbins: I've had the good fortune&nbsp;
of meeting a lot of interesting characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:33.000 --> 02:14:38.440
but there's some that I've spent time with&nbsp;
and there's some that I've just read about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:38.440 --> 02:14:44.947
In the space of famous thieves or con artists,&nbsp;
there is the Unsinkable Titanic Thompson who —&nbsp;

02:14:44.947 --> 02:14:48.360
Tim Ferriss: Titanic Thompson?
Apollo Robbins: Yep. The nickname was Ti,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:48.360 --> 02:14:51.520
from those who knew him well. But there's&nbsp;
books been written about him. I think people&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:51.520 --> 02:14:55.280
have talked about doing movies over the&nbsp;
years. But supposedly he would advertise&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:55.280 --> 02:14:59.560
he was coming to a town, because he gambled&nbsp;
on everything and people were drawn to that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:59.560 --> 02:15:04.658
He was a proposition bet hustler. But he had&nbsp;
super skills. He acquired a lot of skills.&nbsp;

02:15:04.658 --> 02:15:08.560
Tim Ferriss: What's a proposition bet hustler?
Apollo Robbins: Meaning he bet on things that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:08.560 --> 02:15:14.280
would just win. He had special set of skills or&nbsp;
support staff, covert support staff that allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:14.280 --> 02:15:19.200
him to make outrageous bets on weird things.
Tim Ferriss: Can you think of an example?&nbsp;

02:15:19.200 --> 02:15:24.800
Apollo Robbins: One, he would bet just first&nbsp;
on golf, he's very good at golf. And he would&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:24.800 --> 02:15:28.040
play somebody and he'd be beating him and&nbsp;
he says, "Listen, I'll give you a handicap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:28.040 --> 02:15:31.000
I'll play with left-handed." And then he would&nbsp;
beat him again and they didn't realize that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:31.000 --> 02:15:34.667
he was left-handed, but he is that good&nbsp;
that he could beat people right-handed.&nbsp;

02:15:34.667 --> 02:15:37.160
Tim Ferriss: Back to Princess Bride again.
Apollo Robbins: Yes. And so he definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:37.160 --> 02:15:41.680
could switch the hands, but he was very famous&nbsp;
for being able to throw things accurately inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:41.680 --> 02:15:45.880
of cups from a distance. Other things, he could&nbsp;
throw a card from a long ways away through a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:45.880 --> 02:15:50.720
window. And being above a pool hall was one&nbsp;
story where he would be trying to toss a hat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:50.720 --> 02:15:54.760
missing occasionally. And side bets on a poker&nbsp;
game. And he says, "Let's bet a grand that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:54.760 --> 02:15:57.960
can toss a card through that window all the&nbsp;
way down where the window is," and somebody'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:57.960 --> 02:16:00.800
take the bet and throw a card through and then&nbsp;
they'd close down the window, he gets a second&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:00.800 --> 02:16:03.200
bet. Then Kenny, he says, "Listen, I'm going to&nbsp;
throw it through the window, it's going to come&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:03.200 --> 02:16:07.520
back, and it's going to stick to the outside&nbsp;
of the window." What? No, just silly. And it&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:07.520 --> 02:16:12.760
would. And you see those in the literature, but&nbsp;
they're like, "No, that's like magic territory."&nbsp;

02:16:12.760 --> 02:16:15.680
And then he would, somebody would be&nbsp;
eating pretzels and he says, "The pretzel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:15.680 --> 02:16:18.760
I'm going to take that pretzel and throw it&nbsp;
over that three-story building over there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:18.760 --> 02:16:22.960
or that wall line." And they'd say, "No, that&nbsp;
can't." And he would and he'd throw a pretzel&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:22.960 --> 02:16:28.920
over a wall. So he seemed like some kind of Robin&nbsp;
Hood/superhero. But there's great books on him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:28.920 --> 02:16:33.800
the Unsinkable Titanic Thompson. I have the good&nbsp;
fortune of knowing the last surviving member of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:33.800 --> 02:16:38.040
his team that was the guy who was down below in&nbsp;
the pool hall with a fan in rubber cement who&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:38.040 --> 02:16:45.000
painted the window. But he would do amazing things&nbsp;
to layer these very intricate cons besides that.&nbsp;

02:16:45.000 --> 02:16:49.840
So there's a handful of those guys. The&nbsp;
Unsinkable Titanic Thompson, Joseph Weil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:49.840 --> 02:16:56.040
the Yellow Kid. Count Victor Lustig, he's the guy&nbsp;
who sold the Eiffel Tower twice. And it's amazing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:56.040 --> 02:17:00.680
how do you sell the Eiffel Tower? And it is&nbsp;
a beautiful con of, he took advantage of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:00.680 --> 02:17:04.520
time when the Eiffel Tower [did not have] the&nbsp;
significant value that it [would]. It was after&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:04.520 --> 02:17:09.400
World's Fair and it might need to be turned into&nbsp;
scraps. So he put out a bid as a city official&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:09.399 --> 02:17:15.280
to be able to look for somebody who could tear it&nbsp;
down. And then as different contractors bid on it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:15.280 --> 02:17:19.960
he found a guy who really needed the job.
And meanwhile the wife of the guy says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:19.960 --> 02:17:24.120
"I think there's a rat. I smell something. There's&nbsp;
something up here." And he had the beautiful turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:24.120 --> 02:17:29.200
in a con, which he recognized that the wife had&nbsp;
that suspicion, and he answered in a unique way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:29.200 --> 02:17:32.880
He just said, "Listen, I've got to make something&nbsp;
on this. If you guys really want this deal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:32.880 --> 02:17:37.319
I'm going to need a cut." So they decided,&nbsp;
"Okay, hey, that's the rat." That's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:37.319 --> 02:17:41.639
thing. He is suspicious. He's a city official&nbsp;
who needs a cut," instead of "It's a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:41.640 --> 02:17:45.827
fake." So not only did he get the money he was&nbsp;
getting, he also got the extra money as well.&nbsp;

02:17:45.827 --> 02:17:47.054
Tim Ferriss: Oh, man.
Apollo Robbins: So there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:47.640 --> 02:17:53.040
all these guys, all these interesting lessons of&nbsp;
these guys. But I had a personal best friend for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:53.040 --> 02:17:56.704
17 years who — Rod the Hop.
Tim Ferriss: Say again?&nbsp;

02:17:56.704 --> 02:17:58.544
Apollo Robbins: Rod the Hop.
Tim Ferriss: The Hop?&nbsp;

02:17:58.544 --> 02:18:03.600
Apollo Robbins: Yep. And he is significant. If&nbsp;
people have ever watched Ocean's Eleven and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:03.600 --> 02:18:07.880
wondered, "I wonder if there's a real Ocean."&nbsp;
There's a couple of guys that Ocean's character&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:07.880 --> 02:18:12.600
would probably be attributed to, but Rod would be&nbsp;
on that shortlist. Among thieves at least. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:12.600 --> 02:18:16.400
a legend among thieves and people didn't know what&nbsp;
he looked like generally because of the nature&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:16.399 --> 02:18:22.160
of the work. But he played a lot of different&nbsp;
styles. He was a card hustler, card cheat. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:22.160 --> 02:18:28.439
there's a Southern style, a Gardenia style. These&nbsp;
different styles of playing long and short games&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:28.439 --> 02:18:34.559
using devices that shoot out your sleeve and grab&nbsp;
things called the Joint or Kepplinger Holdout.&nbsp;

02:18:34.560 --> 02:18:36.800
He was very good at a lot of those&nbsp;
things. I met him when I first moved&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:36.800 --> 02:18:40.600
to Vegas and he offered — he was sitting off&nbsp;
in a corner. It was at a thing where some&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:40.600 --> 02:18:45.680
magicians were in, he was off the side of that.&nbsp;
And he was playing with plastic cards and he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:45.680 --> 02:18:51.000
shuffling them at the table and not in a way that&nbsp;
magicians handle cards. And he had an edge that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:51.000 --> 02:18:56.227
was a little bit more felt like family to me. And&nbsp;
I saddled up to him, talked to him a little bit.&nbsp;

02:18:56.227 --> 02:19:00.479
Tim Ferriss: So not the way that magicians&nbsp;
handle cards in the sense that you recognize&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:00.479 --> 02:19:05.080
more of like a working card mechanic?
Apollo Robbins: Yeah, because mechanics can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:05.080 --> 02:19:09.960
have flourish. They can't pick up the deck off&nbsp;
the table. They don't shuffle in the same way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:09.960 --> 02:19:15.107
they do. All their slides have to be done within&nbsp;
the constraints of what the casinos have manifest.&nbsp;

02:19:15.107 --> 02:19:16.920
Tim Ferriss: Right.
Apollo Robbins: So it would look&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:16.920 --> 02:19:20.720
very mundane on the surface, but there's a lot&nbsp;
going on there. And also plastic cards, magicians&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:20.720 --> 02:19:25.080
would never want to use plastic cards, but poker&nbsp;
players would. So when I began talking to him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:25.080 --> 02:19:30.584
we kind of hit up a friendship and talked a little&nbsp;
bit. And as he learned more about my past and some&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:30.584 --> 02:19:34.520
of the vibe — I was going through a divorce at the&nbsp;
time too, I was around 21. He knew I needed some&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:34.520 --> 02:19:40.081
money and he said, "Hey, you want to come play&nbsp;
with my team?" And they were hitting casinos or&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:40.080 --> 02:19:46.319
at least side spots, kind of periphery casinos&nbsp;
or gas stations and hitting slot machines and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:46.319 --> 02:19:51.799
things. And he could cheat a slot machine and make&nbsp;
it pay out a bonus. But he needed watch guards. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:51.800 --> 02:19:56.640
needed me to learn a CODY system and stuff. And&nbsp;
I said, "Sorry man, I've grown up around some&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:56.640 --> 02:20:01.000
things like this and there's this line that I know&nbsp;
once I step across that line, I'll always move&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:01.000 --> 02:20:05.960
it. So I can't, man, but I really appreciate it."
He says, "Well, how about all these magic books?&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:05.960 --> 02:20:10.359
What are you going to do with those?" I said, "I&nbsp;
don't know. I guess I might have to sell them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:10.359 --> 02:20:15.040
get this thing for the divorce." And he said,&nbsp;
"Well, I'll buy those from you." So he bought&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:15.040 --> 02:20:20.120
all my magic books from me. And one of his team&nbsp;
turned on him and he ended up getting arrested&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:20.120 --> 02:20:25.880
right after that. And I got this box of books back&nbsp;
with this little note as a friend just saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:25.880 --> 02:20:29.880
"I knew you wouldn't take the money unless I did&nbsp;
